\id PSA - Brenton English Septuagint
\h Psalms 
\toc1 Psalms 
\toc2 Psalms 
\toc3 Ps. 
\mt1 PSALMS 
\c 1 
\q1
\v 1 \sc Blessed\sc* is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly,
\q2 and has not stood in the way of sinners, 
\q2 and has not sat in the seat of \f + \fr 1:1 \fqa Gr. \ft pestilent.\f*evil men. 
\q1
\v 2 But his \f + \fr 1:2 \fqa Gr. \ft will.\f*pleasure is in the law of the Lord; 
\q2 and in his law will he meditate day and night.
\q1
\v 3 He will be like a tree planted by the brooks of waters, 
\q2 which yields its fruit in its season, 
\q2 and its leaf will not fall off; 
\q2 and whatever he does will prosper. 
\q1
\v 4 Not so the ungodly;—not so: 
\q2 but rather as the \f + \fr 1:4 \fqa Or, \ft dust or down.\f*chaff which the wind scatters away from the face of the earth. 
\q1
\v 5 Therefore the ungodly will not rise in judgment, 
\q2 nor sinners in the counsel of the just. 
\q1
\v 6 For the Lord knows the way of the righteous; 
\q2 but the way of the ungodly will perish. 
\c 2 
\q1
\v 1 \x + \xo 2:1 \xt Acts 4. 25.\x* Why did the nations rage,
\q2 and the peoples devise vain things? 
\q1
\v 2 The kings of the earth stood up, 
\q2 and the rulers gathered themselves together, 
\q2 against the Lord, and against his Christ; 
\q1
\v 3 \add saying\add*, Let us break through their bonds, 
\q2 and cast away their yoke from us. 
\q1
\v 4 He who dwells in the heavens will laugh them to scorn, 
\q2 and the Lord will mock them. 
\q1
\v 5 Then he will speak to them in his anger, 
\q2 and trouble them in his fury. 
\q1
\v 6 But I have been made king by him 
\q2 on Zion his holy mountain, 
\q1
\v 7 declaring the ordinance of the Lord: 
\q2 the Lord said to me, \x + \xo 2:7 \xt Acts 13. 33; Heb. 1. 5.\x* You are my Son, 
\q2 today have I begotten you. 
\q1
\v 8 Ask of me, and I will give you the nations \add for\add* your inheritance, 
\q2 and the ends of the earth \add for\add* your possession. 
\q1
\v 9 You will \x + \xo 2:9 \xt Rev. 2. 27.\x*shepherd them with a rod of iron; 
\q2 you will shatter them like a potter's vessel. 
\q1
\v 10 Now therefore understand, you kings: 
\q2 be instructed, all you who judge the earth. 
\q1
\v 11 Serve the Lord with fear, 
\q2 and rejoice in him with trembling. 
\q1
\v 12 Lay hold of instruction, 
\q2 lest the Lord become angry, 
\q2 and you perish from the righteous way: 
\q2 when his wrath is suddenly kindled, 
\q2 blessed are all those who trust in him. 
\c 3 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David, when he fled from the presence of his son Absalom. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, why are those who afflict me multiplied? 
\q2 Many rise up against me. 
\q1
\v 3 Many say concerning my soul, 
\q2 There is no deliverance for him in his God. \f + \fr 3:3 \ft The word Διάψαλμα (Selah) has been rendered Pause, as most intelligible to the English reader.\f*\qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 But you, O Lord, are my helper:
\q2 my glory, and the one who lifts up my head. 
\q1
\v 5 I cried to the Lord with my voice, 
\q2 and he heard me out of his holy mountain. \qs Pause.\qs*
\q1
\v 6 I lay down and slept; 
\q2 I awoke; for the Lord will help me. 
\q1
\v 7 I will not be afraid of ten thousands of people, 
\q2 who beset me all around. 
\q1
\v 8 Arise, Lord; deliver me, my God: 
\q2 for you have struck all who were my enemies without cause; 
\q2 you have broken the teeth of sinners. 
\q1
\v 9 Deliverance is the Lord's, 
\q2 and your blessing is upon your people. 
\c 4 
\d
\v 1 For the end: with Psalms. A Song of David.
\q1
\v 2 When I called,
\q2 the God of my righteousness heard me: 
\q1 in distress you made room for me; 
\q2 pity me, and hear my prayer. 
\q1
\v 3 O you sons of men, 
\q2 how long \add will you be\add* \f + \fr 4:3 \fqa Lit. \ft heavy of heart.\f*slow of heart? 
\q1 Why do you love vanity, 
\q2 and seek falsehood? \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 But know that the Lord has done wondrous things for his holy one: 
\q2 the Lord will hear me when I cry to him. 
\q1
\v 5 \x + \xo 4:5 \xt Eph. 4. 26.\x* Be angry, and do not sin; 
\q2 speak in your hearts, and upon your beds be pierced. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 Offer the sacrifice of righteousness, 
\q2 and trust in the Lord. 
\q1
\v 7 Many say, Who will show us good things? 
\q2 The light of your face, O Lord, has been shown upon us. 
\q1
\v 8 You have put gladness into my heart: 
\q2 from the time their grain and wine and oil increased, they multiplied.
\q1
\v 9 I will \f + \fr 4:9 \fqa Or, \ft at the same time.\f*both lie down in peace and sleep: 
\q2 for you, Lord, only have settled me in hope.
\c 5 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, concerning an heiress.
\q1
\v 2 Listen to my words, O Lord,
\q2 \f + \fr 5:2 \fqa Lit. \ft understand.\f*pay attention to my cry. 
\q1
\v 3 Pay attention to the voice of my supplication, my King, and my God:
\q2 for to you, O Lord, I will pray. 
\q1
\v 4 In the morning you will hear my voice: 
\q2 in the morning I will wait on you, and will look up. 
\q1
\v 5 For you are not a God who desires wickedness; 
\q2 neither will the worker of wickedness dwell with you. 
\q1
\v 6 Neither will the transgressors continue in your sight: 
\q2 you hate, O Lord, all those who work wickedness. 
\q1
\v 7 You will destroy all who speak falsehood: 
\q2 the Lord abhors the man of blood and deceit. 
\q1
\v 8 But I will enter into your house in the multitude of your mercy: 
\q2 In fear of you I will worship toward your holy temple. 
\q1
\v 9 Lead me, O Lord, in your righteousness because of my enemies; 
\q2 make my way plain before your face. 
\q1
\v 10 For there is no truth in their mouth; 
\q2 their heart is vain; 
\q1 their \x + \xo 5:10 \xt Rom. 3. 13.\x* throat is an open sepulchre; 
\q2 with their tongues they have used deceit. 
\q1
\v 11 Judge them, O God; 
\q2 let them fall away from their schemes;
\q1 cast them out according to the abundance of their ungodliness; 
\q2 for they have provoked you, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 12 But let all who trust in you be glad in you: 
\q2 they will exult forever, and you will dwell among them; 
\q2 and all who love your name will rejoice in you. 
\q1
\v 13 For you, Lord, will bless the righteous: 
\q2 you have crowned us as with a shield of favour. 
\c 6 
\d
\v 1 For the end, among the Hymns; for the eighth: a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, don't rebuke me in your wrath, 
\q2 nor chasten me in your anger. 
\q1
\v 3 Pity me, O Lord; for I am weak: 
\q2 heal me, O Lord; for my bones are vexed. 
\q1
\v 4 My soul also is grievously vexed: 
\q2 but you, O Lord, how long? 
\q1
\v 5 Return, O Lord, deliver my soul: 
\q2 save me for your mercy's sake. 
\q1
\v 6 For in death no man remembers you: 
\q2 and who will give you thanks in Hades? 
\q1
\v 7 \f + \fr 6:7 \fqa Gr. \ft I have laboured.\f*I am wearied with my groaning; 
\q2 I will wash my bed every night; 
\q2 I will water my couch with my tears. 
\q1
\v 8 My eye is troubled because of my wrath; 
\q2 I am worn out because of all my enemies. 
\q1
\v 9 Depart from me, all you who work wickedness; 
\q2 for the Lord has heard the voice of my weeping. 
\q1
\v 10 The Lord has listened to my petition; 
\q2 the Lord has accepted my prayer. 
\q1
\v 11 Let all my enemies be put to shame and greatly troubled: 
\q2 let them be turned back and utterly put to shame at once. 
\c 7 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David, which he sang to the Lord because of the words of Chusi the \f + \fr 7:1 \fqa Lit. \ft son of Jemini.\f*Benjamite. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord my God, in you have I trusted:  
\q2 save me from all those who persecute me, and deliver me. 
\q1
\v 3 Lest he seize my soul like a lion,  
\q2 while there is no one to redeem, nor to save. 
\q1
\v 4 O Lord my God, if I have done this, 
\q2 if there is unrighteousness in my hands, 
\q1
\v 5 if I have repayed with evil those who repayed me, 
\q2 then may I fall empty before my enemies. 
\q1
\v 6 Let the enemy persecute my soul, and take it;  
\q2 and let him trample my life on the ground,  
\q2 and lay my glory in the dust. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 Arise, O Lord, in your wrath;  
\q2 be exalted at the boundaries of my enemies:  
\q2 awake, O Lord my God, according to the decree which you commanded. 
\q1
\v 8 The congregation of the peoples will surround you:  
\q2 and because of this, return on high. 
\q1
\v 9 The Lord will judge the nations:  
\q2 judge me, O Lord, according to my righteousness,  
\q2 and according to my innocence that is in me. 
\q1
\v 10 Oh let the wickedness of sinners come to an end;  
\q2 and you will guide the righteous,  
\q2 O God who searches the hearts and kidneys. 
\q1
\v 11 My righteous help is from God,  
\q2 who saves the upright in heart. 
\q1
\v 12 God is a righteous judge, and strong, and patient,  
\q2 not inflicting vengeance every day. 
\q1
\v 13 If you will not repent, he will sharpen his sword;  
\q2 he has strung his bow, and made it ready. 
\q1
\v 14 On it he prepared instruments of death;  
\q2 he has fashioned his arrows for the burning ones. 
\q1
\v 15 Behold, he was in labour with unrighteousness,  
\q2 he has conceived trouble, and brought forth wickedness. 
\q1
\v 16 He has opened a pit, and dug it up,  
\q2 and he will fall into the ditch which he has made. 
\q1
\v 17 His trouble will return on his own head,  
\q2 and his unrighteousness will come down on his own crown. 
\q1
\v 18 I will give thanks to the Lord according to his righteousness;  
\q2 I will sing to the name of the Lord most high. 
\c 8 
\d
\v 1 For the end, concerning the wine-presses, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, our Lord, how wonderful is your name in all the earth!  
\q2 For your magnificence is exalted above the heavens. 
\q1
\v 3 \x + \xo 8:3 \xt Mat. 21. 16.\x* Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings you have established praise, because of your enemies;   
\q2 that you might put down the enemy and avenger. 
\q1
\v 4 For I will regard the heavens, the work of your fingers;   
\q2 the moon and stars, which you have established. 
\q1
\v 5 \x + \xo 8:5 \xt Heb. 2. 6-9.\x* What is man, that you are mindful of him?   
\q2 Or the son of man, that you care for him? 
\q1
\v 6 You made him a little less than angels,   
\q2 you have crowned him with glory and honour; 
\q1
\v 7 and you have set him over the works of your hands:   
\q2 you have put all things under his feet: 
\q1
\v 8 sheep and all oxen,   
\q2 yes, and the beasts of the field; 
\q1
\v 9 the birds of the sky, and the fish of the sea,   
\q2 whatever passes through the seas' paths. 
\q1
\v 10 O Lord our Lord, how wonderful is your name in all the earth! 
\c 9 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, concerning the secrets of the Son. 
\q1
\v 2 I will give thanks to you, O Lord, with my whole heart; 
\q2 I will recount all your wonderful works. 
\q1
\v 3 I will be glad and exult in you: 
\q2 I will sing to your name, O Most High. 
\q1
\v 4 When my enemies are turned back, 
\q2 they will be feeble and perish at your presence. 
\q1
\v 5 For you have maintained my cause and my right; 
\q2 you sat on the throne, who judges justly. 
\q1
\v 6 You have rebuked the nations, and the ungodly one has perished; 
\q2 you have blotted out their name forever, 
\q2 even forever and ever. 
\q1
\v 7 The swords of the enemy have failed completely; 
\q2 and you have destroyed cities: 
\q2 their memorial has been destroyed with a noise, 
\q1
\v 8 but the Lord endures forever: 
\q2 he has prepared his throne for judgment. 
\q1
\v 9 He will judge the world in righteousness, 
\q2 he will judge the nations in uprightness. 
\q1
\v 10 The Lord also has become a refuge for the poor, 
\q2 a timely help in affliction. 
\q1
\v 11 Let those who know your name hope in you: 
\q2 for you, O Lord, have not failed those who diligently seek you. 
\q1
\v 12 Sing praises to the Lord, who dwells in Zion: 
\q2 declare his dealings among the nations. 
\q1
\v 13 For seeking out their blood, he remembered:
\q2 he has not forgotten the cry of the poor. 
\q1
\v 14 Have mercy upon me, O Lord; 
\q2 see my humiliation from my enemies, 
\q2 you who lift me up from the gates of death: 
\q1
\v 15 that I may declare all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion: 
\q2 I will exult in your salvation. 
\q1
\v 16 The nations are caught in the destruction which they planned: 
\q2 their foot is taken in the very snare which they hid.
\q1
\v 17 The Lord is known by executing judgments: 
\q2 the sinner was caught in the works of his hands. \qs A song of pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 18 Let sinners be driven away into Hades, 
\q2 all the nations that forget God. 
\q1
\v 19 For the poor will not be forgotten forever: 
\q2 the patience of the needy ones will not perish forever. 
\q1
\v 20 Arise, O Lord, don't let man prevail: 
\q2 let the nations be judged before you. 
\q1
\v 21 Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them: 
\q2 let the nations know that they are men. \qs Pause.\qs*

\q1
\v 22 \f + \fr 9:22 \fqa Note.\ft —Here begins a change of numbers as compared with the \fqa Heb. \ft which continues to Ps. 146, this psalm having no number, and the eleventh being called the tenth. The following verses 22-39 are numbered 1-18 in the original text.\f*Why do you stand far off, O Lord, 
\q2 looking away in times of affliction? 
\q1
\v 23 While the ungodly one acts proudly, the poor person burns:
\q2 they are caught in the schemes they devise.
\q1
\v 24 Because the sinner praises himself for the desires of his heart; 
\q2 and the unjust one blesses himself. 
\q1
\v 25 The sinner has provoked the Lord: 
\q2 according to the abundance of his anger he will not seek:
\q2 God isn't before him. 
\q1
\v 26 His ways are corrupted at all times; 
\q2 your judgments are removed from before him: 
\q2 he will rule over all his enemies. 
\q1
\v 27 For he has said in his heart, I will not be moved, 
\q2 from generation to generation free from harm.\x + \xo 9:27 \xt Rom. 8. 14.\x* 
\q1
\v 28 Whose mouth is full of cursing, and bitterness, and fraud: 
\q2 under his tongue are trouble and pain. 
\q1
\v 29 He lies in wait with the rich in secret places, in order to kill the innocent: 
\q2 his eyes are fixed on the poor person. 
\q1
\v 30 He lies in wait in secret like a lion in his den: 
\q2 he lies in wait to seize the poor person, to seize the poor person when he draws him in:
\q1
\v 31 he will bring him down in his snare;
\q2 he crouches and falls when he gains mastery over the poor. 
\q1
\v 32 For he has said in his heart, God has forgotten: 
\q2 he has turned away his face so as to never look. 
\q1
\v 33 Arise, O Lord God; let your hand be lifted up: 
\q2 don't forget the poor. 
\q1
\v 34 Why has the wicked provoked God? 
\q2 For he has said in his heart, He will not call to account.
\q1
\v 35 You see; for you observe trouble and rage, to deliver them into your hands: 
\q2 the poor person has been left to you; you were a helper to the orphan. 
\q1
\v 36 Break the arm of the wicked sinner:
\q2 his sin will be sought out, and \f + \fr 9:36 \fqa Gr. \ft οὐ μὴ εὑρεθῇ δι’ αὐτήν ``will surely not be found because of it''. It is unclear what the antecedant of `it' is.\f*will not be found. 
\q1
\v 37 The Lord will reign forever, even forever and ever: 
\q2 you nations will perish out of his land. 
\q1
\v 38 The Lord has heard the desire of the poor: 
\q2 your ear has inclined to the preparation of their heart; 
\q1
\v 39 to plead for the orphan and afflicted, 
\q2 that man may no more boast upon the earth. 
\c 10 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\v 1b In the Lord I have put my trust: 
\q2 how will you say to my soul, Flee to the mountains as a sparrow? 
\q1
\v 2 For behold the sinners have strung the bow, 
\q2 they have made ready their arrows in the quiver, 
\q2 to shoot in the dark of the moon at the upright in heart. 
\q1
\v 3 For they have pulled down what you established; 
\q2 but what has the righteous one done? 
\q1
\v 4 The Lord is in his holy temple, 
\q2 the Lord, his throne is in heaven: 
\q2 his eyes look upon the poor person, 
\q2 his eyelids scrutinise the sons of men. 
\q1
\v 5 The Lord tries the righteous and the ungodly: 
\q2 band he who loves unrighteousness hates his own soul. 
\q1
\v 6 He will rain down snares, fire, and brimstone upon sinners,
\q2 and a stormy blast \add will be\add* the portion of their cup. 
\q1
\v 7 For the Lord is righteous, and loves \f + \fr 10:7 \fqa Gr. \ft righteousnesses.\f*righteousness; 
\q2 his face beholds uprightness. 
\c 11 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, upon the eighth. 
\q1
\v 2 Save me, O Lord; for the godly man has failed; 
\q2 for truths have become rare among the children of men. 
\q1
\v 3 Every one has spoken vanity to his neighbour: 
\q2 their lips are deceitful, they have spoken with a double heart. 
\q1
\v 4 Let the Lord destroy all the deceitful lips, 
\q2 and the tongue that speaks great words: 
\q1
\v 5 who have said, We will magnify our tongue; 
\q2 our lips are our own: who is Lord of us? 
\q1
\v 6 Because of the misery of the poor, 
\q2 and because of the sighing of the needy, 
\q2 now I will arise, says the Lord, 
\q2 I will set \add them\add* in safety; 
\q2 I will speak boldly in it. 
\q1
\v 7 The words of the Lord are pure words; 
\q2 as silver refined, 
\q2 tested in \f + \fr 11:7 \fqa Gr. \ft the earth.\f*a furnace of earth, 
\q2 purified seven times. 
\q1
\v 8 You, O Lord, will keep us, 
\q2 and will preserve us, 
\q2 from this generation, and forever. 
\q1
\v 9 The ungodly walk around: 
\q2 according to your greatness you have \f + \fr 11:9 \ft greatly exalted: the Greek is obscure here.\f*greatly exalted the sons of men. 
\c 12 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 How long, O Lord, will you forget me? Forever? 
\q2 How long will you turn your face away from me? 
\q1
\v 3 How long will I take counsel in my soul,
\q2 sorrows in my heart daily? 
\q2 How long will my enemy be exalted over me? 
\q1
\v 4 Look on me, listen to me, O Lord my God: 
\q2 lighten my eyes, lest I sleep in death; 
\q1
\v 5 lest at any time my enemy say, I have prevailed against him: 
\q2 my persecutors will exult if ever I should be moved. 
\q1
\v 6 But I have hoped in your mercy; 
\q2 my heart will exult in your salvation.
\q1
\v 6b I will sing to the Lord who has dealt bountifully with me, 
\q2 and I will sing psalms to the name of the Lord most high. 
\c 13 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David.
\q1
\v 1b The fool has said in his heart, There is no God. 
\q2 They have corrupted \add themselves\add*, 
\q2 and become abominable in their devices; 
\q2 there is no one that does goodness, 
\q2 there isn't even so much as one. 
\q1
\v 2 The Lord looked down from heaven upon the sons of men, 
\q2 to see if there were any that understood, or sought after God. 
\q1
\v 3 They have all gone out of the way, 
\q2 they have together become good for nothing, 
\q2 there is no one who does good, no not one. 
\q1 Their throat is an open sepulchre; 
\q2 with their tongues they have used deceit; 
\q1 the poison of asps is under their lips: 
\q2 whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness; 
\q1 their feet are swift to shed blood: 
\q2 destruction and misery are in their ways; 
\q2 and the way of peace they have not known: 
\q1 there is no fear of God before their eyes. 
\q1
\v 4 Will not all the workers of wickedness know, 
\q2 who eat up my people as they would eat bread? 
\q2 They have not called upon the Lord. 
\q1
\v 5 There were they seized with fear where there was no fear; 
\q2 for God is among the generation of the righteous. 
\q1
\v 6 You have shamed the counsel of the poor, 
\q2 because the Lord is his hope. 
\q1
\v 7 Who will bring the salvation of Israel out of Zion? 
\q2 When the Lord brings back the captivity of his people, 
\q3 let Jacob exult, and Israel be glad. 
\c 14 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David.
\q1
\v 1b O Lord, who will sojourn in your tabernacle? 
\q2 Who will dwell in your holy mountain? 
\q1
\v 2 He who walks blameless, 
\q2 and works righteousness, 
\q2 who speaks truth in his heart. 
\q1
\v 3 Who has not spoken craftily with his tongue, 
\q2 nor done evil to his neighbour, 
\q2 nor taken up a reproach against those who dwelt nearest to him. 
\q1
\v 4 In his sight an evil-worker is despised, 
\q2 but he honours those who fear the Lord. 
\q2 He swears to his neighbour, 
\q2 and doesn't disappoint. 
\q1
\v 5 He has not \f + \fr 14:5 \fqa Gr. \ft given.\f*lent his money on usury, 
\q2 and has not received bribes against the innocent. 
\q2 He who does these things will never be moved. 
\c 15 
\d
\v 1 A writing of David. 
\q1
\v 1b Keep me, O Lord; for I have hoped in you. 
\q1
\v 2 I said to the Lord, You are my Lord; 
\q2 for you have no need of my \f + \fr 15:2 \fqa Gr. \ft good deeds, or things.\f*goodness. 
\q1
\v 3 On behalf of the saints that are in his land, 
\q2 he has magnified all his purposes in them. 
\q1
\v 4 Their afflictions have been multiplied; 
\q2 afterward they hastened; 
\q1 I will surely not assemble their assemblies of blood,
\q2 neither will I make mention of their names with my lips. 
\q1
\v 5 The Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup: 
\q2 you are he who restores my inheritance to me. 
\q1
\v 6 The lines have fallen to me in the best places, 
\q2 yes, I have a most excellent heritage. 
\q1
\v 7 I will bless the Lord who has instructed me; 
\q2 my kidneys too have disciplined me even \f + \fr 15:7 \fqa Possibly, \ft during the night. \fqa q. d. \ft as long as night lasted.\f*till night. 
\q1
\v 8 I saw the Lord always before me;
\q2 for he is at my right hand, that I should not be moved. 
\q1
\v 9 Therefore my heart rejoiced and my tongue exulted; 
\q2 moreover also my flesh will rest in hope: 
\q1
\v 10 because you will not leave my soul in Hades, 
\q2 neither will you allow your holy one to see corruption. 
\q1
\v 11 You have made known to me the ways of life; 
\q2 you will fill me with joy before your face: 
\q2 at your right hand \add there are\add* delights forever. 
\c 16 
\d
\v 1 A Prayer of David. 
\v 1b Listen, O Lord \f + \fr 16:1 \fqa Or, \ft to my righteous plea, etc.\f*of my righteousness, 
\q2 pay attention to my petition; 
\q2 give ear to my prayer not from deceitful lips. 
\q1
\v 2 Let my judgment come forth from your presence; 
\q2 let my eyes behold righteousness. 
\q1
\v 3 You have proved my heart; 
\q2 you have visited \add me\add* by night; 
\q2 you have tried me by fire, and unrighteousness has not been found in me, 
\q1
\v 4 so that my mouth might not speak the works of men;
\q2 because of the words of your lips I have kept hard ways.
\q1
\v 5 Direct my steps in your paths, 
\q2 that my steps not slip. 
\q1
\v 6 I have cried, for you heard me, O God: 
\q2 incline your ear to me, and listen to my words. 
\q1
\v 7 Make your mercies marvellous;
\q2 with your right hand you who save those who hope in you from those who resist. 
\q1
\v 8 Keep me as the apple of the eye;
\q2 you will cover me in the shelter of your wings, 
\q1
\v 9 from the face of the ungodly who have afflicted me: 
\q2 my enemies have surrounded my soul. 
\q1
\v 10 They have enclosed their fat: 
\q2 their mouth has spoken pride. 
\q1
\v 11 They have now cast me out and surrounded me:
\q2 they have set their eyes to bring \add me\add* down to the ground. 
\q1
\v 12 They seized me like a lion ready for prey, 
\q2 and like a lion's cub dwelling in secret places.
\q1
\v 13 Arise, O Lord, forestall them, and trip them up: 
\q2 deliver my soul from the ungodly: 
\q2 your sword: 
\q1
\v 14 from the enemies of your hand. 
\q2 O Lord, destroy them from the earth; 
\q2 scatter them in their life,
\q2 though their belly has been filled with your hidden things:
\q2 they have been satisfied with children,
\q2 and have left their remaining things to their babes. 
\q1
\v 15 But I will appear in righteousness before your face: 
\q2 I will be satisfied when your glory appears. 
\c 17 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of David, the servant of the Lord, who spoke to the Lord the words of this song in the day in which the Lord delivered him out of the hand of all his enemies, and out of the hand of Saul: and he said: 
\q1
\v 2 I will love you, O Lord, my strength. 
\q1
\v 3 The Lord is my firm support, and my refuge, and my deliverer; 
\q2 my God is my helper, I will hope in him; 
\q2 my defender, and the horn of my salvation, and my helper. 
\q1
\v 4 I will call upon the Lord with praises, 
\q2 and I will be saved from my enemies. 
\q1
\v 5 The pangs of death surrounded me, 
\q2 and the torrents of ungodliness troubled me exceedingly. 
\q1
\v 6 The pangs of Hades came all around me: 
\q2 the snares of death overtook me. 
\q1
\v 7 When I was afflicted I called upon the Lord, 
\q2 and cried to my God: 
\q2 he heard my voice out of his holy temple, 
\q2 and my cry will come before him, into his ears. 
\q1
\v 8 Then the earth shook and quaked, 
\q2 and the foundations of the mountains were disturbed, and were shaken, 
\q2 because God was angry with them. 
\q1
\v 9 There went up a smoke in his wrath, 
\q2 and fire burst into a flame at his presence: 
\q2 coals were kindled at it. 
\q1
\v 10 He bent the heavens, and came down: 
\q2 and thick darkness was under his feet. 
\q1
\v 11 He mounted on cherubs and flew: 
\q2 he flew on the wings of winds. 
\q1
\v 12 He made darkness his secret place: 
\q2 all around him was his tabernacle: 
\q2 dark water in the clouds of the air. 
\q1
\v 13 At the brightness before him the clouds passed, 
\q2 hail and coals of fire. 
\q1
\v 14 The Lord also thundered from heaven, 
\q2 and the Highest uttered his voice. 
\q1
\v 15 He sent arrows, and scattered them; 
\q2 and multiplied lightnings, and routed them. 
\q1
\v 16 The springs of waters appeared, 
\q2 and the foundations of the world were exposed, 
\q2 at your rebuke, O Lord, 
\q2 at the blasting of the breath of your wrath. 
\q1
\v 17 He sent from on high and took me, 
\q2 he drew me to himself out of many waters. 
\q1
\v 18 He will deliver me from my mighty enemies, 
\q2 and from those who hate me; 
\q2 for they are stronger than I. 
\q1
\v 19 They overtook me in the day of my affliction: 
\q2 but the Lord was my support.
\q1
\v 20 He brought me out into a wide place: 
\q2 he will deliver me, because he has pleasure in me. 
\q1
\v 21 The Lord will recompense me according to my righteousness; 
\q2 even according to the purity of my hands he will recompense me. 
\q1
\v 22 For I have kept the ways of the Lord 
\q2 and have not wickedly departed from my God. 
\q1
\v 23 For all his judgments were before me, 
\q2 and his ordinances didn't depart from me. 
\q1
\v 24 I will be blameless with him, 
\q2 and will keep myself from my wickedness. 
\q1
\v 25 The Lord will recompense me according to my righteousness, 
\q2 and according to the purity of my hands before his eyes. 
\q1
\v 26 With the holy you will be holy; 
\q2 and with the innocent man you will be innocent. 
\q1
\v 27 With the excellent you will be excellent; 
\q2 and with the crooked you will show yourself crooked.
\q1
\v 28 For you will save the lowly people, 
\q2 and will humble the eyes of the proud. 
\q1
\v 29 For you, O Lord, will light my lamp: 
\q2 my God, you will lighten my darkness. 
\q1
\v 30 For by you I will be delivered from a troop; 
\q2 and by my God I will pass over a wall. 
\q1
\v 31 \add As for\add* my God, his way is perfect: 
\q2 the words of the Lord are tried in the fire; 
\q2 he is a protector of all those who hope in him. 
\q1
\v 32 For who is God but the Lord? 
\q2 And who is a God except our God? 
\q1
\v 33 God, who girds me with strength, 
\q2 and has made my way blameless: 
\q1
\v 34 who strengthens my feet as deer's feet, 
\q2 and sets me upon high places. 
\q1
\v 35 He instructs my hands for war; 
\q2 and you have made my arms a bronze bow. 
\q1
\v 36 You gave me the protection of my salvation: 
\q2 your right hand has helped me, 
\q2 and your discipline has upheld me to the end; 
\q2 yes, your discipline itself will teach me. 
\q1
\v 37 You have widened my steps beneath me, 
\q2 and my footsteps did not falter. 
\q1
\v 38 I will pursue my enemies, and overtake them; 
\q2 and I will not turn back until they are consumed. 
\q1
\v 39 I will dash them to pieces and they will not be able to stand: 
\q2 they will fall under my feet. 
\q1
\v 40 For you have girded me with strength for war: 
\q2 you have beaten down under me all who rose up against me. 
\q1
\v 41 You have made my enemies turn their backs before me; 
\q2 and you have destroyed those who hated me. 
\q1
\v 42 They cried, but there was no deliverer: 
\q2 to the Lord, but he didn't listen to them. 
\q1
\v 43 I will grind them as the mud of the streets: 
\q2 and I will beat them small as dust before the wind. 
\q1
\v 44 Deliver me from the contentions of the people: 
\q2 you will make me head of the nations:
\q2  a people whom I didn't know served me, 
\q1
\v 45 \f + \fr 17:45 \fqa Gr. \ft at the hearing of the ear.\f*as soon as they heard they obeyed me:
\q2 the sons of foreigners feigned obedience to me. 
\q1
\v 46 The sons of foreigners grew old, 
\q2 and limped from their paths.
\q1
\v 47 The Lord lives; and blessed be my God; 
\q2 and let the God of my salvation be exalted, 
\q1
\v 48 God, who avenges me, 
\q2 and subdues the nations under me; 
\q1
\v 49 my deliverer from angry enemies:
\q2  you will lift me on high above those who rise up against me: 
\q2 you will deliver me from the unrighteous man. 
\q1
\v 50 Therefore will I acknowledge you, O Lord, among the nations,
\q2 and sing to your name,
\q1
\v 51 magnifying the deliverances of his king; 
\q2 and showing mercy to his anointed, 
\q2 to David and his seed forever. 
\c 18 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 The heavens declare the glory of God; 
\q2 and the firmament proclaims the work of his hands. 
\q1
\v 3 Day to day pours forth speech, 
\q2 and night to night proclaims knowledge. 
\q1
\v 4 There are no speeches or words
\q2 whose voices are not heard. 
\q1
\v 5 \x + \xo 18:5 \xt Rom. 10. 18.\x*Their voice has gone out into all the earth, 
\q2 and their words to the ends of the world. 
\q1
\v 6 In the sun he has set his tent; 
\q2 and he comes forth as a bridegroom out of his chamber: 
\q2 he will exult like a strong man to run his course. 
\q1
\v 7 His going forth is from the extremity of heaven, 
\q2 and his \f + \fr 18:7 \fqa Gr. \ft meeting.\f*circuit to the other end of heaven: 
\q2 and no one will be hidden from his heat. 
\q1
\v 8 The law of the Lord is \f + \fr 18:8 \fqa Gr. \ft spotless.\f*perfect, restoring souls: 
\q2 the testimony of the Lord is faithful, making babes wise. 
\q1
\v 9 The ordinances of the Lord are right, rejoicing the heart: 
\q2 the commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes. 
\q1
\v 10 The fear of the Lord is pure, enduring forever and ever: 
\q2 the judgments of the Lord are true, righteous altogether. 
\q1
\v 11 To be desired more than gold, and much precious stone: 
\q2 sweeter also than honey and the honey-comb. 
\q1
\v 12 For your servant keeps to them: 
\q2 in the keeping of them is great reward. 
\q1
\v 13 Who can understand transgressions? 
\q2 Purge me from my hidden faults.
\q1
\v 14 Spare your servant from strangers: 
\q2 if they do not gain dominion over me, then I will be blameless, 
\q2 and I will be clear from great sin. 
\q1
\v 15 So will the words of my mouth, and the meditation of my heart, be continually pleasing before you, 
\q2 O Lord my helper, and my redeemer. 
\c 19 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 The Lord hear you in the day of trouble; 
\q2 the name of the God of Jacob defend you, 
\q1
\v 3 send you help from the sanctuary, 
\q2 and aid you out of Zion, 
\q1
\v 4 remember all your \f + \fr 19:4 \fqa Or, \ft meat offering.\f*sacrifices, 
\q2 and accept your whole-burnt-offering, \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 grant you according to your heart, 
\q2 and fulfil all your counsel. 
\q1
\v 6 We will exult in your salvation, 
\q2 and in the name of our God we will be magnified: 
\q2 the Lord fulfil all your petitions. 
\q1
\v 7 Now I know that the Lord has saved his Christ: 
\q2 he will hear him from his holy heaven: 
\q2 the salvation of his right hand is mighty. 
\q1
\v 8 These in chariots, and these in horses: 
\q2 but we, in the name of the Lord our God we will be magnified. 
\q1
\v 9 They were tripped up and fell: 
\q2 but we rose and have been raised upright. 
\q1
\v 10 O Lord, save the king: 
\q2 and hear us in whatever day we call upon you. 
\c 20 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, the king will rejoice in your strength; 
\q2 and in your salvation he will greatly exult. 
\q1
\v 3 You have granted him the desire of his soul, 
\q2 and have not withheld from him the request of his lips. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 For you have prevented him with blessings of goodness: 
\q2 you have set upon his head a crown of precious stone. 
\q1
\v 5 He asked life of you, 
\q2 and you gave him length of days forever and ever. 
\q1
\v 6 His glory is great in your salvation: 
\q2 you will crown him with glory and majesty. 
\q1
\v 7 For you will give him a blessing forever and ever: 
\q2 you will gladden him with joy with your face. 
\q1
\v 8 For the king trusts in the Lord, 
\q2 and through the mercy of the Highest he will not be moved. 
\q1
\v 9 Let your hand be found by all your enemies: 
\q2 let your right hand find all who hate you. 
\q1
\v 10 You will make them as a fiery oven at the time of your presence: 
\q2 the Lord will trouble them in his anger, and fire will devour them. 
\q1
\v 11 You will destroy their fruit from the earth, 
\q2 and their seed from the sons of men. 
\q1
\v 12 For they intended evils against you; 
\q2 they imagined a device which they will by no means be able to perform. 
\q1
\v 13 For you will make them \add turn their\add* back,
\q2 with your remnants you will prepare their face. 
\q1
\v 14 Be exalted, O Lord, in your strength: 
\q2 we will sing and praise your mighty acts. 
\c 21 
\d
\v 1 For the end, concerning the morning aid, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 \x + \xo 21:2 \xt Mat. 27. 46.\x*O God, my God, pay attention to me: 
\q2 why have you forsaken me? 
\q2 The words of my transgressions are far from my deliverance. 
\q1
\v 3 O my God, I will cry to you by day, but you will not hear: 
\q2 and by night, and it is not folly to me. 
\q1
\v 4 But you dwell among the holy ones, O praise of Israel.
\q1
\v 5 Our fathers hoped in you; 
\q2 they hoped, and you delivered them. 
\q1
\v 6 They cried to you, and were saved: 
\q2 they hoped in you, and were not ashamed. 
\q1
\v 7 But I am a worm, and not a man; 
\q2 a reproach of men, and scorn of the people. 
\q1
\v 8 All who saw me mocked me: 
\q2 they spoke with their lips, they shook the head, saying, 
\q1
\v 9 He hoped in the Lord: let him deliver him, 
\q2 let him save him, because he takes pleasure in him. 
\q1
\v 10 For you are he who drew me out of the womb; 
\q2 my hope from my mother's breasts. 
\q1
\v 11 I was cast on you from the womb: 
\q2 you are my God from my mother's belly. 
\q1
\v 12 Don't stand aloof from me; 
\q2 for affliction is near; 
\q2 for there is no helper. 
\q1
\v 13 Many young bulls have surrounded me: 
\q2 fat bulls have hemmed me in.
\q1
\v 14 They have opened their mouth against me, 
\q2 like a ravening and roaring lion. 
\q1
\v 15 I am poured out like water, and all my bones are scattered: 
\q2 my heart has become like melting wax within me. 
\q1
\v 16 My strength is dried up like a potsherd; 
\q2 and my tongue is glued to my throat; 
\q2 and you have brought me down to the dust of death. 
\q1
\v 17 For many dogs have surrounded me: 
\q2 the assembly of the evildoers has hemmed me in:
\q2 they pierced my hands and my feet. 
\q1
\v 18 I counted all my bones; 
\q2 and they observed and looked upon me. 
\q1
\v 19 \x + \xo 21:19 \xt Mat. 27. 35.\x* They parted my garments among themselves, 
\q2 and cast lots upon my clothing. 
\q1
\v 20 But you, O Lord, don't put my help far from me: 
\q2 be ready for my aid. 
\q1
\v 21 Deliver my soul from the sword; 
\q2 my only-begotten one from the power of the dog. 
\q1
\v 22 Save me from the lion's mouth; 
\q2 and my lowliness from the horns of the  \f + \fr 21:22 \fqa Gr. \ft “one-horned” (μονοκερώτων), traditionally “unicorns”; probably referring to powerful wild oxen or similar animals.\f*wild oxen. 
\q1
\v 23 I will declare your name to my brothers: 
\q2 among the congregation I will sing praise to you. 
\q1
\v 24 You who fear the Lord, praise him; 
\q2 all you seed of Jacob, glorify him: 
\q2 let all the seed of Israel fear him. 
\q1
\v 25 For he has not despised nor been angry at the supplication of the poor; 
\q2 nor turned away his face from me; 
\q2 but when I cried to him, he heard me. 
\q1
\v 26 My praise is of you in the great congregation: 
\q2 I will pay my vows before those who fear him. 
\q1
\v 27 The poor will eat and be satisfied; 
\q2 and those who seek him will praise the Lord:
\q2 their hearts will live forever. 
\q1
\v 28 All the ends of the earth will remember and turn to the Lord: 
\q2 and all the families of the nations will worship before you. 
\q1
\v 29 For the kingdom is the Lord's; 
\q2 and he rules the nations. 
\q1
\v 30 All the fat ones of the earth have eaten and worshipped: 
\q2 all who go down to the earth will fall down before him: 
\q2 my soul also lives for him. 
\q1
\v 31 My seed will serve him: 
\q2 the generation that is coming will be proclaimed to the Lord. 
\q1
\v 32 They will proclaim his righteousness to the people who will be born, 
\q2 whom the Lord has made. 
\c 22 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 1b The Lord tends me as a shepherd, 
\q2 and I will want nothing. 
\q1
\v 2 In a place of green grass, there he has made me dwell: 
\q2 he has nourished me by the water of rest. 
\q1
\v 3 He has restored my soul: 
\q2 he has guided me into the paths of righteousness, 
\q2 for his name's sake. 
\q1
\v 4 Yes, even if I should walk among the shadow of death, I will not be afraid of evils: 
\q2 for you are with me; 
\q2 your rod and your staff, these have comforted me. 
\q1
\v 5 You have prepared a table before me in presence of those who afflict me: 
\q2 you have anointed my head with oil; 
\q2 and your cup exhilarates me like the best \add wine\add*. 
\q1
\v 6 Your mercy also will follow me all the days of my life: 
\q2 and my dwelling in the house of the Lord unto length of days.
\c 23 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm for David on the first day of the week. 
\q1
\v 1b The earth is the Lord's and the fulness of it; 
\q2 the world, and all who dwell in it. 
\q1
\v 2 He has founded it upon the seas, 
\q2 and prepared it upon the rivers. 
\q1
\v 3 Who will go up to the mountain of the Lord, 
\q2 and who will stand in his holy place? 
\q1
\v 4 He who is innocent in his hands and pure in his heart; 
\q2 who has not lifted up his soul to vanity, nor sworn deceitfully \f + \fr 23:4 \fqa Or, \ft against.\f*to his neighbour. 
\q1
\v 5 He will receive a blessing from the Lord, 
\q2 and mercy from God his Saviour. 
\q1
\v 6 This is the generation of those who seek him, 
\q2 that seek the face of the God of Jacob. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 Lift up your gates, you princes, 
\q2 and be lifted up, you everlasting doors; 
\q2 and the king of glory will come in. 
\q1
\v 8 Who is this king of glory? 
\q2 The Lord strong and mighty, 
\q2 the Lord mighty in battle. 
\q1
\v 9 Lift up your gates, you princes; 
\q2 and be lift up, you everlasting doors; 
\q2 and the king of glory will come in. 
\q1
\v 10 Who is this king of glory? 
\q2 The Lord of hosts, he is this king of glory. 
\c 24 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 1b To you, O Lord, have I lifted up my soul. 
\q1
\v 2 O my God, I have trusted in you: 
\q2 don't let me be greatly agitated, 
\q2 nor let my enemies laugh me to scorn. 
\q1
\v 3 For surely no one who waits on you will be ashamed: 
\q2 let them be ashamed who transgress without cause. 
\q1
\v 4 Show me your ways, O Lord; 
\q2 and teach me your paths. 
\q1
\v 5 Lead me in your truth, and teach me: 
\q2 for you are God my Saviour: 
\q2 and I have waited on you all day. 
\q1
\v 6 Remember your compassions, O Lord, and your mercies, 
\q2 for they are from everlasting. 
\q1
\v 7 Don't remember the sins of my youth or of my ignorance: 
\q2 remember me according to your mercy, for your goodness' sake, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 8 Good and upright is the Lord: 
\q2 therefore he will establish law for sinners in the way. 
\q1
\v 9 He will guide the meek in judgment: 
\q2 he will teach the meek his ways. 
\q1
\v 10 All the ways of the Lord are mercy and truth 
\q2 to those who seek his covenant and his testimonies. 
\q1
\v 11 For your name's sake, O Lord, be merciful to my sin; 
\q2 for it is great. 
\q1
\v 12 Who is the man who fears the Lord? 
\q2 He will instruct him in the way which he has chosen. 
\q1
\v 13 His soul will dwell in prosperity; 
\q2 and his seed will inherit the earth. 
\q1
\v 14 The Lord is the strength of those who fear him; 
\q2 \f + \fr 24:14 \ft Some Greek copies add, “and the name of the Lord for those who fear him.”\f*and his covenant is for making known to them.
\q1
\v 15 My eyes are continually to the Lord; 
\q2 for he will draw my feet out of the snare. 
\q1
\v 16 Look on me, and have mercy on me; 
\q2 for I am an only child and poor. 
\q1
\v 17 The afflictions of my heart have been multiplied; 
\q2 deliver me from my distresses. 
\q1
\v 18 Look upon my affliction and my trouble; 
\q2 and forgive all my sins. 
\q1
\v 19 Look upon my enemies; for they have been multiplied; 
\q2 and they have hated me with unjust hatred. 
\q1
\v 20 Keep my soul, and deliver me: 
\q2 don't let me be ashamed; 
\q2 for I have hoped in you. 
\q1
\v 21 The harmless and upright joined themselves to me: 
\q2 for I waited for you, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 22 Deliver Israel, O God, 
\q2 out of all his afflictions. 
\c 25 
\d
\v 1 Of David. 
\q1
\v 1b Judge me, O Lord; for I have walked in my innocence: 
\q2 and hoping in the Lord I will not be moved. 
\q1
\v 2 Prove me, O Lord, and try me; 
\q2 purify as with fire my kidneys and my heart. 
\q1
\v 3 For your mercy is before my eyes: 
\q2 and I am well pleased with your truth. 
\q1
\v 4 I have not sat with the council of vanity, 
\q2 and will not enter in with transgressors. 
\q1
\v 5 I have hated the assembly of evildoers; 
\q2 and will not sit with the ungodly. 
\q1
\v 6 I will wash my hands among the innocent,
\q2 and surround your altar, O Lord: 
\q1
\v 7 to hear the voice of praise, 
\q2 and to declare all your wonderful works. 
\q1
\v 8 O Lord, I have loved the beauty of your house, 
\q2 and the place of the tabernacle of your glory. 
\q1
\v 9 Don't destroy my soul together with the ungodly, 
\q2 nor my life with bloody men: 
\q1
\v 10 in whose hands are iniquities;
\q2 their right hand is filled with bribes. 
\q1
\v 11 But I have walked in my innocence: 
\q2 redeem me, and have mercy upon me. 
\q1
\v 12 My foot stands in uprightness: 
\q2 in the congregations I will bless you, O Lord. 
\c 26 
\d
\v 1 Of David, before he was anointed. 
\q1
\v 1b The Lord is my light and my Saviour; 
\q2 whom will I fear? 
\q2 The Lord is the defender of my life; 
\q2 of whom will I be afraid? 
\q1
\v 2 When evil-doers drew near against me 
\q2 to eat up my flesh, 
\q2 my persecutors and my enemies, 
\q2 they fainted and fell. 
\q1
\v 3 Though an army should set itself in array against me, 
\q2 my heart will not be afraid: 
\q2 though war should rise up against me, 
\q2 in this am I confident. 
\q1
\v 4 One thing have I asked of the Lord, 
\q2 this will I earnestly seek: 
\q2 that I should dwell in the house of the Lord 
\q2 all the days of my life, 
\q2 that I should behold the fair beauty of the Lord, 
\q2 and survey his temple. 
\q1
\v 5 For in the day of my afflictions 
\q2 he hid me in his tabernacle: 
\q2 he sheltered me in the hidden place of his tabernacle; 
\q2 he set me up on a rock. 
\q1
\v 6 Now, behold, he has lifted up my head over my enemies: 
\q2 I circled and offered in his tabernacle the sacrifice of shouting;
\q2 I will sing and make melody to the Lord. 
\q1
\v 7 Hear, O Lord, my voice which I have uttered aloud: 
\q2 pity me, and listen to me. 
\q1
\v 8 My heart said to you, I have diligently sought your face: 
\q2 your face, O Lord, I will seek. 
\q1
\v 9 Don't turn your face away from me, 
\q2 don't turn away from your servant in anger: 
\q2 be my helper, don't cast me away; 
\q2 and, O God my Saviour, don't forsake me.
\q1
\v 10 For my father and my mother have forsaken me, 
\q2 but the Lord has taken me to himself. 
\q1
\v 11 Teach me, O Lord, in your way, 
\q2 and guide me in a right path, 
\q2 because of my enemies. 
\q1
\v 12 Don't deliver me over to the desire of those who afflict me; 
\q2 for unjust witnesses have risen up against me, 
\q2 and injustice has lied to herself. 
\q1
\v 13 I believe that I will see the Lord's good things
\q2 in the land of the living. 
\q1
\v 14 Wait on the Lord: 
\q2 be of good courage, and let your heart be strengthened: 
\q2 yes, wait on the Lord. 
\c 27 
\d
\v 1 Of David. 
\q1
\v 1b To you, O Lord, have I cried; 
\q2 my God, don't keep silent toward me: 
\q2 lest you keep silent toward me, 
\q2 and I should become like those who go down to the pit. 
\q1
\v 2 Listen to the voice of my supplication, 
\q2 when I pray to you, 
\q2 when I lift up my hands toward your holy temple. 
\q1
\v 3 Don't draw away my soul with sinners, 
\q2 and don't destroy me with the workers of wickedness,
\q2 who speak peace with their neighbours,
\q2 but evil things are in their hearts. 
\q1
\v 4 Give them according to their works, 
\q2 and according to the wickedness of their devices: 
\q2 give them according to the works of their hands; 
\q2 give their recompence to them. 
\q1
\v 5 Because they have not attended to the works of the Lord, 
\q2 even to the works of his hands, 
\q2 you will pull them down, 
\q2 and will not build them up. 
\q1
\v 6 Blessed be the Lord, 
\q2 for he has listened to the voice of my petition. 
\q1
\v 7 The Lord is my helper and my defender; 
\q2 my heart has hoped in him, and I was helped: 
\q2 my flesh has revived, and from my own desire I will acknowledge and praise him.
\q1
\v 8 The Lord is the strength of his people, 
\q2 and the defender of the deliverances of his anointed. 
\q1
\v 9 Save your people, 
\q2 and bless your inheritance: 
\q2 and take care of them, 
\q2 and lift them up forever. 
\c 28 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David: for the departure of the tabernacle. 
\q1
\v 1b Bring to the Lord, you sons of God, 
\q2 bring to the Lord sons of rams; 
\q2 bring to the Lord glory and honour. 
\q1
\v 2 Bring to the Lord glory, \add due\add* to his name; 
\q2 worship the Lord in his holy court. 
\q1
\v 3 The voice of the Lord is upon the waters: 
\q2 the God of glory has thundered: 
\q2 the Lord is upon many waters. 
\q1
\v 4 The voice of the Lord is mighty; 
\q2 the voice of the Lord is full of majesty. 
\q1
\v 5 The voice of the Lord breaking the cedars; 
\q2 the Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon. 
\q1
\v 6 He will beat them small, \add even\add* Lebanon itself, like a calf; 
\q2 and the beloved one is as a son of \f + \fr 28:6 \fqa Gr. \ft “one-horned” (μονοκερώτων), traditionally “unicorns”; probably referring to a powerful wild ox or similar animal.\f*wild oxen. 
\q1
\v 7 The voice of the Lord dividing a flame of fire. 
\q1
\v 8 The voice of the Lord shaking the wilderness; 
\q2 the Lord will shake the wilderness of Cades. 
\q1
\v 9 The voice of the Lord strengthening the deer, 
\q2 and will uncover the thickets: 
\q2 and in his temple every one says Glory. 
\q1
\v 10 The Lord will dwell on the waterflood: 
\q2 and the Lord will sit as king forever. 
\q1
\v 11 The Lord will give strength to his people; 
\q2 the Lord will bless his people with peace. 
\c 29 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm \f + \fr 29:1 \fqa Gr. \ft of a song.\f*and Song at the dedication of the house. Of David. 
\q1
\v 2 I will exalt you, O Lord; for you have lifted me up,
\q2 and not caused my enemies to rejoice over me. 
\q1
\v 3 O Lord my God, I cried to you, and you did heal me. 
\q1
\v 4 O Lord, you have brought up my soul from Hades, 
\q2 you have delivered me from among those who go down to the pit. 
\q1
\v 5 Sing to the Lord, you his saints, 
\q2 and give thanks for the remembrance of his holiness. 
\q1
\v 6 For anger is in his wrath, 
\q2 but life in his favour: 
\q2 weeping will tarry for the evening, 
\q2 but joy will be in the morning. 
\q1
\v 7 I said in my prosperity, I will never be moved. 
\q1
\v 8 O Lord, in your good pleasure you added strength to my beauty: 
\q2 but you turned away your face, and I was troubled. 
\q1
\v 9 To you, O Lord, I will cry; 
\q2 and to my God I will make supplication. 
\q1
\v 10 What profit is there in my blood, 
\q2 when I go down to destruction? 
\q2 Will the dust give praise to you? 
\q2 Or will it declare your truth? 
\q1
\v 11 The Lord heard, and had compassion upon me; 
\q2 the Lord has become my helper. 
\q1
\v 12 You have turned my mourning into joy for me: 
\q2 you have torn off my sackcloth, and clothed me with gladness; 
\q1
\v 13 that my glory may sing praise to you, 
\q2 and I may not be pierced. 
\q2 O Lord my God, I will give thanks to you forever. 
\c 30 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David, of \f + \fr 30:1 \fqa Gr. \ft ἐκστάσεως has a broad semantic range and might mean ecstasy, astonishment, bewilderment, terror/panic, anguish, etc. It has the idea of being ``beside oneself'' with strong emotion.\f*dismay. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, I have hoped in you; 
\q2 let me never be ashamed: 
\q2 deliver me in your righteousness and rescue me. 
\q1
\v 3 Incline your ear to me; 
\q2 make haste to rescue me: 
\q2 be to me for a protecting God, 
\q2 and for a house of refuge to save me. 
\q1
\v 4 For you are my strength and my refuge; 
\q2 and you will guide me for your name's sake, and maintain me. 
\q1
\v 5 You will bring me out of the snare which they have hidden for me; 
\q2 for you, O Lord, are my defender. 
\q1
\v 6 Into your hands I will commit my spirit: 
\q2 you have redeemed me, O Lord God of truth. 
\q1
\v 7 You have hated those who idly persist in vanities: 
\q2 but I have hoped in the Lord. 
\q1
\v 8 I will exult and be glad in your mercy: 
\q2 for you have looked upon my affliction; 
\q2 you have saved my soul from distresses. 
\q1
\v 9 You have not shut me up into the hands of the enemy: 
\q2 you have set my feet in a wide place. 
\q1
\v 10 Pity me, O Lord, for I am afflicted: 
\q2 my eye is troubled with indignation, 
\q2 my soul and my belly. 
\q1
\v 11 For my life is spent with grief, 
\q2 and my years with groanings: 
\q2 my strength has been weakened through poverty, 
\q2 and my bones are troubled. 
\q1
\v 12 I became a reproach among all my enemies, 
\q2 but exceedingly so to my neighbours, 
\q2 and a fear to my acquaintance: 
\q2 those who saw me outside fled from me. 
\q1
\v 13 I have been forgotten as a dead man out of mind: 
\q2 I have become as a broken vessel. 
\q1
\v 14 For I heard the slander of many that dwelt all around: 
\q2 when they were gathered together against me,
\q2 they took counsel to take my life. 
\q1
\v 15 But I hoped in you, O Lord: 
\q2 I said, You are my God. 
\q1
\v 16 My times are in your hands: 
\q2 deliver me from the hand of my enemies, 
\q1
\v 17 and from those who persecute me. 
\q2 Make your face to shine upon your servant: 
\q2 save me in your mercy. 
\q1
\v 18 O Lord, don't let me be ashamed, for I have called upon you: 
\q2 let the ungodly be ashamed, and brought down to Hades. 
\q1
\v 19 Let the deceitful lips become dumb, 
\q2 which speak wickedness against the righteous with pride and scorn. 
\q1
\v 20 How abundant is the multitude of your goodness, O Lord, 
\q2 which you have laid up for those who fear you! 
\q2 You have worked \add it\add* out for those who hope on you, 
\q2 in the presence of the sons of men. 
\q1
\v 21 You will hide them in the hidden place of your presence from the turmoil of men: 
\q2 you will screen them in a tent from the contradiction of tongues. 
\q1
\v 22 Blessed be the Lord: 
\q2 for he has magnified his mercy in a fortified city. 
\q1
\v 23 But I said in my \f + \fr 30:23 \fqa Gr. \ft ἐκστάσεως has a broad semantic range and might mean ecstasy, astonishment, bewilderment, terror/panic, anguish, etc. It has the idea of being ``beside oneself'' with strong emotion.\f*dismay, I am cast out from the sight of your eyes: 
\q2 therefore you listened, O Lord, to the voice of my supplication when I cried to you. 
\q1
\v 24 Love the Lord, all you his saints: 
\q2 for the Lord seeks out truth, 
\q2 and renders \add a reward\add* to those who deal very proudly. 
\q1
\v 25 Be of good courage, 
\q2 and let your heart be strengthened, 
\q2 all you who hope in the Lord. 
\c 31 
\d
\v 1 Of David. For instruction.
\q1
\v 1b \x + \xo 31:1 \xt Rom. 4. 7,8.\x*Blessed \add are they\add* whose transgressions are forgiven,
\q2 and whose sins are covered. 
\q1
\v 2 Blessed is the man to whom the Lord will not impute sin, 
\q2 and in whose mouth there is no guile. 
\q1
\v 3 Because I kept silence, my bones grew old, 
\q2 from my crying all the day. 
\q1
\v 4 For day and night your hand was heavy upon me: 
\q2 I was turned into misery while a thorn was driven in. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 I acknowledged my sin, and didn't hide my wickedness: 
\q2 I said, I will confess my wickedness to the Lord against myself; 
\q2 and you forgave the ungodliness of my heart. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 Therefore every holy one will pray to you at a fitting time: 
\q2 only in the deluge of many waters they will not come near to him. 
\q1
\v 7 You are my refuge from the affliction that encompasses me; 
\q2 my joy, to deliver me from those who have surrounded me. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 8 I will instruct you and guide you in the way you will go:
\q2 I will fix my eyes upon you. 
\q1
\v 9 Don't be like horse and mule, which have no understanding: 
\q2 constrain their jaws with bit and bridle, lest they should come near to you. 
\q1
\v 10 Many are the scourges of the sinner: 
\q2 but mercy will surround him that hopes in the Lord.
\q1
\v 11 Be glad in the Lord, and exult, you righteous: 
\q2 and glory, all you who are upright in heart. 
\c 32 
\d
\v 1 Of David. 
\q1
\v 1b Rejoice in the Lord, you righteous; 
\q2 praise becomes the upright. 
\q1
\v 2 Give thanks to the Lord on the harp; 
\q2 play to him on a psaltery of ten strings. 
\q1
\v 3 Sing to him a new song; 
\q2 play skilfully with a loud noise. 
\q1
\v 4 For the word of the Lord is right; 
\q2 and all his works are in faithfulness.
\q1
\v 5 He loves mercy and judgment; 
\q2 the earth is full of the mercy of the Lord. 
\q1
\v 6 By the word of the Lord the heavens were established; 
\q2 and all their host by the breath of his mouth.
\q1
\v 7 Who gathers the waters of the sea as \add in\add* a wineskin; 
\q2 who lays up the deeps in treasuries. 
\q1
\v 8 Let all the earth fear the Lord; 
\q2 and let all who dwell in the world be shaken because of him. 
\q1
\v 9 For he spoke, and they were made; 
\q2 he commanded, and they were created. 
\q1
\v 10 The Lord frustrates the counsels of the nations; 
\q2 he brings to nought also the reasonings of the peoples, 
\q2 and brings to nought the counsels of princes. 
\q1
\v 11 But the counsel of the Lord endures forever, 
\q2 the thoughts of his heart from generation to generation. 
\q1
\v 12 Blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord; 
\q2 the people whom he has chosen for his own inheritance. 
\q1
\v 13 The Lord looks out of heaven; 
\q2 he beholds all the sons of men. 
\q1
\v 14 He looks from his prepared habitation 
\q2 on all the dwellers on the earth; 
\q1
\v 15 who fashioned their hearts individually;
\q2 who understands all their works. 
\q1
\v 16 A king isn't saved because of a great host; 
\q2 and a mighty man will not be delivered by the greatness of his strength. 
\q1
\v 17 A horse is vain for safety; 
\q2 neither he will be delivered by the greatness of his power. 
\q1
\v 18 Behold, the eyes of the Lord are on those who fear him, 
\q2 those that hope in his mercy; 
\q1
\v 19 to deliver their souls from death, 
\q2 and to keep them alive in famine. 
\q1
\v 20 Our soul waits on the Lord; 
\q2 for he is our helper and defender. 
\q1
\v 21 For our heart will rejoice in him, 
\q2 and we have hoped in his holy name. 
\q1
\v 22 Let your mercy, O Lord, be upon us, 
\q2 as we have hoped in you. 
\c 33 
\d
\v 1 Of David, when he changed his face before Abimelech; and he let him go, and he departed. 
\q1
\v 2 I will bless the Lord at all times:  
\q2 his praise will be continually in my mouth. 
\q1
\v 3 My soul will glory in the Lord:  
\q2 let the meek hear, and rejoice. 
\q1
\v 4 Magnify the Lord with me,  
\q2 and let us exalt his name together. 
\q1
\v 5 I sought the Lord diligently,  
\q2 and he listened to me,  
\q2 and delivered me from all my sojournings. 
\q1
\v 6 Draw near to him, and be enlightened:  
\q2 and your faces will surely not be ashamed. 
\q1
\v 7 This poor man cried, and the Lord listened to him,  
\q2 and delivered him out of all his afflictions. 
\q1
\v 8 The angel of the Lord will encamp all around those who fear him,  
\q2 and will deliver them. 
\q1
\v 9 Taste and see that the Lord is good:  
\q2 blessed is the man who hopes in him. 
\q1
\v 10 Fear the Lord, all you his saints:  
\q2 for there is no want to those who fear him. 
\q1
\v 11 The rich have become poor and hungry:  
\q2 but those who seek the Lord diligently will not want any good thing. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 12 Come, you children, hear me:  
\q2 I will teach you the fear of the Lord. 
\q1
\v 13 \x + \xo 33:13 \xt 1 Pet. 3. 10-13.\x*What man is there that desires life,  
\q2 loving to see good days? 
\q1
\v 14 Keep your tongue from evil,  
\q2 and your lips from speaking guile. 
\q1
\v 15 Turn away from evil, and do good;  
\q2 seek peace, and pursue it. 
\q1
\v 16 The eyes of the Lord are over the righteous,  
\q2 and his ears to their prayer: 
\q1
\v 17 but the face of the Lord is against those who do evil,  
\q2 to destroy their memorial from the earth.  
\q1 The righteous cried, and the Lord listened to them, 
\q2
\v 18 and delivered them out of all their afflictions. 
\q1
\v 19 The Lord is near to those who are broken in heart;  
\q2 and will save the lowly in spirit. 
\q1
\v 20 Many are the afflictions of the righteous:  
\q2 but out of them all the Lord will deliver them. 
\q1
\v 21 He keeps all their bones:  
\q2 not one of them will be broken. 
\q1
\v 22 The death of sinners is evil:  
\q2 and those who hate the righteous one will be guilty.
\q1
\v 23 The Lord will redeem the souls of his servants:  
\q2 and none of those who hope in him will be guilty.
\c 34 
\d
\v 1 Of David.
\q1
\v 1b Judge, O Lord, those who injure me, 
\q2 fight against those who fight against me. 
\q1
\v 2 Take hold of shield and buckler, 
\q2 and arise for my help. 
\q1
\v 3 Bring forth a sword, 
\q2 and block \add the way\add* against those who persecute me: 
\q2 say to my soul, I am your salvation. 
\q1
\v 4 Let those who seek my soul be ashamed and greatly agitated: 
\q2 let those who devise evils against me be turned back and put to shame. 
\q1
\v 5 Let them be as dust before the wind, 
\q2 and an angel of the Lord afflicting them. 
\q1
\v 6 Let their way be dark and slippery, 
\q2 and an angel of the Lord persecuting them. 
\q1
\v 7 For without cause they have hidden their destructive snare for me: 
\q2 without cause they reproached me.
\q1
\v 8 Let a snare which they don't know come upon them: 
\q2 and the trap which they hid take them; 
\q2 and let them fall into that very snare. 
\q1
\v 9 But my soul will exult in the Lord: 
\q2 it will delight in his salvation. 
\q1
\v 10 All my bones will say, O Lord, who is like to you? 
\q2 Delivering the poor out of the hand of those who are stronger than he, 
\q2 yes, the poor and needy one from those who spoil him. 
\q1
\v 11 Unjust witnesses arose, 
\q2 and asked me about things I didn't know. 
\q1
\v 12 They rewarded me evil for good, 
\q2 and bereavement to my soul. 
\q1
\v 13 But I, when they troubled me, put on sackcloth, 
\q2 and humbled my soul with fasting: 
\q2 and my prayer will return to my bosom. 
\q1
\v 14 As a neighbour, as a brother I behaved kindly towards them:
\q2 I humbled myself as one mourning and sad of face. 
\q1
\v 15 Yet they rejoiced against me,
\q2 and gathered together;
\q2 scourges gathered together against me, 
\q2 and I didn't know \add it\add*: 
\q2 they were torn apart, but were not \f + \fr 34:15 \ft See \xt Acts 2. 37.\f*pierced \add in heart\add*.
\q1
\v 16 They tempted me, 
\q2 they sneered at me most contemptuously, 
\q2 they gnashed their teeth at me. 
\q1
\v 17 O Lord, when will you look upon me? 
\q2 Deliver my soul from their mischief, 
\q2 my only-begotten one from the lions. 
\q1
\v 18 I will give thanks to you even in a great congregation: 
\q2 in an abundant people I will praise you. 
\q1
\v 19 Don't let those who are my enemies without a cause rejoice against me; 
\q2 who hate me for nothing, and wink with their eyes. 
\q1
\v 20 For they spoke peaceably to me, 
\q2 but imagined deceits in \add their\add* anger. 
\q1
\v 21 They opened wide their mouth upon me; 
\q2 they said, Aha, aha, our eyes have seen \add it\add*. 
\q1
\v 22 You have seen, O Lord: don't keep silence: 
\q2 O Lord, don't withdraw from me. 
\q1
\v 23 Awake, O Lord, and pay attention to my judgment, 
\q2 to my cause, my God and my Lord. 
\q1
\v 24 Judge me, O Lord, according to your righteousness, 
\q2 O Lord my God; and don't let them rejoice against me. 
\q1
\v 25 Don't let them say in their hearts, Aha, aha, our soul's desire! 
\q2 Neither let them say, We have devoured him. 
\q1
\v 26 Let them be greatly agitated and ashamed together that rejoice at my afflictions: 
\q2 let them be clothed with shame and confusion that speak great swelling words against me. 
\q1
\v 27 Let those who rejoice in my righteousness exult and be glad: 
\q2 and let them say continually, The Lord be magnified, who desire the peace of his servant. 
\q1
\v 28 My tongue will meditate on your righteousness, 
\q2 on your praise all the day. 
\c 35 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of David, the servant of the Lord. 
\q1
\v 2 The transgressor, that he may sin, says within himself,
\q2 \x + \xo 35:2 \xt Rom. 3. 18.\x*there is no fear of God before his eyes. 
\q1
\v 3 For he has dealt deceitfully before him, 
\q2 to discover his wickedness and hate it. 
\q1
\v 4 The words of his mouth are transgression and deceit: 
\q2 he isn't willing to understand \add how\add* to do good. 
\q1
\v 5 He devises wickedness on his bed; 
\q2 he gives himself to every evil way; 
\q2 and does not abhor evil. 
\q1
\v 6 O Lord, your mercy is in heaven; 
\q2 and your truth is to the clouds. 
\q1
\v 7 Your righteousness is as the \f + \fr 35:7 \fqa Or, \ft vast mountains.\f*mountains of God, 
\q2 your judgments are as a great deep: 
\q2 O Lord, you will preserve men and beasts. 
\q1
\v 8 How have you multiplied your mercy, O God! 
\q2 So the children of men will trust in the shelter of your wings. 
\q1
\v 9 They will be drunk with the richness of your house; 
\q2 and you will cause them to drink of the full stream of your delights. 
\q1
\v 10 For with you is the fountain of life: 
\q2 in your light we will see light. 
\q1
\v 11 Extend your mercy to those who know you; 
\q2 and your righteousness to the upright in heart. 
\q1
\v 12 Don't let the foot of pride come against me, 
\q2 and don't let the hand of sinners move me. 
\q1
\v 13 There all the workers of wickedness have fallen: 
\q2 they were driven out, and will not be able to stand. 
\c 36 
\d
\v 1 Of David.
\q1
\v 1b Don't fret because of evil-doers, 
\q2 nor be envious of those who do wickedness. 
\q1
\v 2 For they will soon be withered like grass, 
\q2 and will soon fall away like green plants. 
\q1
\v 3 Hope in the Lord, and do good; and dwell on the land, 
\q2 and you will be fed with the wealth of it. 
\q1
\v 4 Delight in the Lord; 
\q2 and he will grant you the requests of your heart. 
\q1
\v 5 Disclose your way to the Lord, and hope in him; 
\q2 and he will do it.
\q1
\v 6 He will bring forth your righteousness as the light, 
\q2 and your judgment as the noon-day. 
\q1
\v 7 Submit yourself to the Lord, and entreat him: 
\q2 don't fret because of him who prospers in his way, 
\q2 at the man who does unlawful deeds. 
\q1
\v 8 Cease from anger, and forsake wrath: 
\q2 don't fret so as to do evil. 
\q1
\v 9 For evil-doers will be destroyed: 
\q2 but those who wait on the Lord, they will inherit the land. 
\q1
\v 10 In just a little while the sinner will not be, 
\q2 and you will seek for his place, and will not find \add it\add*. 
\q1
\v 11 But the meek will inherit the earth; 
\q2 and will delight in the abundance of peace. 
\q1
\v 12 The sinner will watch for the righteous, 
\q2 and gnash his teeth at him. 
\q1
\v 13 But the Lord will laugh at him: 
\q2 for he foresees that his day will come. 
\q1
\v 14 Sinners have drawn their sword, 
\q2 they have strung their bow, 
\q1 to cast down the poor and needy one, 
\q2 to kill the upright in heart. 
\q1
\v 15 Let their sword enter their \add own\add* heart, 
\q2 and their bows be broken. 
\q1
\v 16 A little is better to the righteous 
\q2 than abundant wealth of sinners. 
\q1
\v 17 For the arms of sinners will be broken; 
\q2 but the Lord supports the righteous. 
\q1
\v 18 The Lord knows the ways of the blameless; 
\q2 and their inheritance will be forever. 
\q1
\v 19 They will not be ashamed in an evil time; 
\q2 and in days of famine they will be satisfied. 
\q1
\v 20 For the sinners will perish; 
\q2 and the enemies of the Lord, at the very time they are honoured and exalted, vanish; they vanished like smoke. 
\q1
\v 21 The sinner borrows, and will not repay: 
\q2 but the righteous has compassion, and gives. 
\q1
\v 22 For those who bless him will inherit the earth; 
\q2 and those who curse him will be completely destroyed. 
\q1
\v 23 The steps of a man are rightly ordered by the Lord: 
\q2 and he will take pleasure in his way. 
\q1
\v 24 When he falls, he will not be ruined: 
\q2 for the Lord supports his hand. 
\q1
\v 25 I have been young and am now old; 
\q2 yet I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread. 
\q1
\v 26 He is merciful, and lends continually; 
\q2 and his seed will be for a blessing.
\q1
\v 27 Turn aside from evil, and do good; 
\q2 and dwell forever. 
\q1
\v 28 For the Lord loves justice, and will not forsake his saints; 
\q2 they will be preserved forever: 
\q2 \f + \fr 36:28 \ft Some Greek texts: ``the blameless will be vindicated''.\f*but the lawless will be driven out,
\q2 and the seed of the ungodly will be completely destroyed. 
\q1
\v 29 But the righteous will inherit the earth, 
\q2 and dwell upon it forever. 
\q1
\v 30 The mouth of the righteous will meditate wisdom, 
\q2 and his tongue will speak of justice. 
\q1
\v 31 The law of his God is in his heart; 
\q2 and his steps will not be tripped up. 
\q1
\v 32 The sinner watches the righteous, 
\q2 and seeks to kill him. 
\q1
\v 33 But the Lord will surely not leave him in his hands, 
\q2 and will surely not condemn him when he is judged. 
\q1
\v 34 Wait on the Lord, and keep his way, 
\q2 and he will exalt you to inherit the land: 
\q2 when the wicked are destroyed, you will see \add it\add*. 
\q1
\v 35 I saw the ungodly very highly exalting himself, 
\q2 and lifting himself up like the cedars of Lebanon. 
\q1
\v 36 Yet I passed by, and lo! He was not: 
\q2 and I sought him, but his place was not found. 
\q1
\v 37 Maintain innocence, and behold uprightness: 
\q2 for there is a remnant for the peaceable man. 
\q1
\v 38 But the transgressors will be completely destroyed together: 
\q2 the remnants of the ungodly will be completely destroyed. 
\q1
\v 39 But the salvation of the righteous is from the Lord; 
\q2 and he is their defender in the time of affliction. 
\q1
\v 40 The Lord will help them, and deliver them: 
\q2 and he will rescue them from sinners, and save them, 
\q2 because they have hoped in him. 
\c 37 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David. For remembrance concerning the Sabbath. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, don't rebuke me in your wrath, 
\q2 don't discipline me in your anger. 
\q1
\v 3 For your weapons are fixed in me, 
\q2 and you have pressed your hand heavily upon me. 
\q1
\v 4 For there is no health in my flesh because of your anger; 
\q2 there is no peace to my bones because of my sins. 
\q1
\v 5 For my transgressions have gone over my head: 
\q2 they have pressed heavily upon me like a weighty burden. 
\q1
\v 6 My wounds have festered and rotted, 
\q2 because of my foolishness. 
\q1
\v 7 I have been wretched and bowed down continually: 
\q2 I went with a mourning face all the day. 
\q1
\v 8 For my \f + \fr 37:8 \ft Some Greek texts substitute ἡ ψυχή μου ``my soul'' for αἱ ψύαι μου ``my loins''.\f*loins are filled with mocking; 
\q2 and there is no health in my flesh. 
\q1
\v 9 I have been afflicted and brought down exceedingly: 
\q2 I have roared for the groaning of my heart. 
\q1
\v 10 But all my desire is before you; 
\q2 and my groaning isn't hidden from you. 
\q1
\v 11 My heart is troubled, my strength has left me; 
\q2 and the light of my eyes has also left me. 
\q1
\v 12 My friends and my neighbours drew near and stood before me;
\q2 and those closest to me stood far off. 
\q1
\v 13 While those who sought my life pressed hard upon me:
\q2 and those who sought my harm spoke vanities, and devised deceits all the day. 
\q1
\v 14 But I, as a deaf man, didn't hear; 
\q2 and was as a mute man not opening his mouth. 
\q1
\v 15 I was as a man who doesn't hear, 
\q2 and who has no reproofs in his mouth. 
\q1
\v 16 For I hoped in you, O Lord: 
\q2 you will hear, O Lord my God. 
\q1
\v 17 For I said, Lest my enemies rejoice against me: 
\q2 for when my feet were moved, they spoke boastingly against me. 
\q1
\v 18 For I am ready for scourges, 
\q2 and my grief is continually before me. 
\q1
\v 19 For I will declare my wickedness, 
\q2 and be distressed for my sin. 
\q1
\v 20 But my enemies live, and are mightier than I: 
\q2 and those who hate me unjustly are multiplied. 
\q1
\v 21 Those who reward evil for good slandered me; 
\q2 because I followed righteousness. 
\q1
\v 22 Don't forsake me, O Lord my God: 
\q2 don't depart from me. 
\q1
\v 23 Draw near to my help, 
\q2 O Lord of my salvation. 
\c 38 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Song of David. To Idithun. 
\q1
\v 2 I said, I will take heed to my ways, 
\q2 that I not sin with my tongue: 
\q2 I set a guard on my mouth, 
\q2 while the sinner stood in my presence. 
\q1
\v 3 I became deaf, and humbled myself, 
\q2 and kept silence from good \add things\add*; 
\q2 and my grief was renewed. 
\q1
\v 4 My heart grew hot within me, 
\q2 and a fire would kindle in my meditation: 
\q2 I spoke with my tongue, 
\q1
\v 5 O Lord, make me to know my end, 
\q2 and the number of my days, what it is;
\q2 that I may know what I lack. 
\q1
\v 6 Behold, you have made my days a handbreadth long;
\q2 and my existence is as nothing before you: 
\q2 surely every man living is altogether vanity. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 Surely man walks in an image; 
\q2 no, they are disquieted in vain: 
\q2 he lays up treasures, 
\q2 and doesn't know for whom he will gather them. 
\q1
\v 8 Now what is my expectation? 
\q2 Is it not the Lord? 
\q2 And my existence is with you. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 Deliver me from all my transgressions: 
\q2 you have made me a reproach to the foolish. 
\q1
\v 10 I became deaf, 
\q2 and didn't open my mouth; 
\q2 for you are he who made me. 
\q1
\v 11 Remove your scourges from me: 
\q2 I have fainted because of the strength of your hand. 
\q1
\v 12 You chasten man with rebukes for wickedness, 
\q2 and you have wasted away his life like a spider:
\q2 no, every man is disquieted in vain. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 13 O Lord, listen to my prayer and my supplication: 
\q2 pay attention to my tears: 
\q2 don't be silent, for I am a sojourner in the land, 
\q2 and a stranger, as all my fathers were.
\q1
\v 14 Spare me, that I may be refreshed, 
\q2 before I depart, and exist no more. 
\c 39 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 I waited and waited for the Lord; 
\q2 and he attended to me, 
\q2 and listened to my plea. 
\q1
\v 3 He brought me up out of a pit of misery, 
\q2 and from miry clay: 
\q2 and he set my feet on a rock, 
\q2 and directed my steps.
\q1
\v 4 He put a new song into my mouth, a hymn to our God: 
\q2 many will see, and fear, and will hope in the Lord. 
\q1
\v 5 Blessed is the man whose hope is the name of the Lord, 
\q2 and who has not regarded vanities and false frenzies. 
\q1
\v 6 O Lord my God, you have multiplied your wonderful works, 
\q2 and in your thoughts there is no one who will be likened to you: 
\q2 I declared and spoke \add of them\add*: 
\q2 they exceeded number. 
\q1
\v 7 \x + \xo 39:7 \xt Heb. 10. 5,6.\x*Sacrifice and offering you didn't desire; 
\q2 but you have prepared \f + \fr 39:7 \fqa Gr. \ft ὠτία "ears"; some Greek texts have σῶμα "a body".\f*ears for me: 
\q2 whole-burnt-offering and \add sacrifice\add* for sin you did not require. 
\q1
\v 8 Then I said, Behold, I have come: 
\q2 in the scroll of the book it is written concerning me, 
\q1
\v 9 I desired to do your will, O my God, 
\q2 and your law is \f + \fr 39:9 \fqa Gr. \ft within my belly.\f*within me.
\q1
\v 10 I have preached righteousness in the great congregation; 
\q2 lo! I will not refrain my lips; O Lord, you know.
\q1
\v 11 I did not hide your righteousness within my heart, 
\q2 and I have declared your truth and your salvation; 
\q2 I did not hide your mercy and your truth from the great congregation. 
\q1
\v 12 But you, Lord, don't remove your compassion far from me; 
\q2 your mercy and your truth have helped me continually. 
\q1
\v 13 For innumerable evils have encompassed me; 
\q2 my transgressions have taken hold of me, and I could not see; 
\q2 they are multiplied more than the hairs of my head; 
\q2 and my heart has failed me. 
\q1
\v 14 O Lord, be well pleased to deliver me; 
\q2 O Lord, draw near to help me. 
\q1
\v 15 Let those who seek my life to destroy it be ashamed and disgraced together; 
\q2 let those who wish me evil be turned backward and put to shame. 
\q1
\v 16 Let those who say to me, Aha, aha, quickly receive shame for their reward. 
\q1
\v 17 Let all those who seek you, O Lord, exult and rejoice in you; 
\q2 and let those who love your salvation say continually, The Lord be magnified. 
\q1
\v 18 But I am poor and needy; 
\q2 the Lord will take care of me; 
\q2 you are my helper, and my defender, 
\q2 O my God, don't delay. 
\c 40 
\d
\v 1 For the end, a Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 Blessed is the one who considers the poor and needy: 
\q2 the Lord will deliver him in an evil day. 
\q1
\v 3 May the Lord preserve him and keep him alive, 
\q2 and bless him \f + \fr 40:3 \fqa Or, \ft in the land.\f*on the earth, 
\q2 and not deliver him into the hands of his enemy. 
\q1
\v 4 May the Lord help him on his bed of pain; 
\q2 you have turned his whole bed in his sickness. 
\q1
\v 5 I said, O Lord, have mercy upon me; 
\q2 heal my soul; for I have sinned against you. 
\q1
\v 6 My enemies have spoken evil against me, 
\q2 \add saying\add*, When will he die, and his name perish? 
\q1
\v 7 If he came to see \add me\add*, he spoke vainly; 
\q2 his heart gathered wickedness for itself; 
\q2 he went out and spread it abroad. 
\q1
\v 8 All my enemies whispered against me; 
\q2 against me they devised my harm. 
\q1
\v 9 They denounced a wicked word against me, \add saying\add*, 
\q2 Will the one who lies down ever rise again? 
\q1
\v 10 For even the man of my peace, in whom I trusted, 
\q2 \x + \xo 40:10 \xt John 13. 18.\x*who ate my bread, lifted up \add his\add* heel against me. 
\q1
\v 11 But you, O Lord, have compassion upon me, 
\q2 and raise me up, and I will repay them. 
\q1
\v 12 By this I know that you have delighted in me, 
\q2 because my enemy will not rejoice over me. 
\q1
\v 13 But you helped me because of \add my\add* innocence, 
\q2 and have established me before you forever. 
\b
\q1
\v 14 Blessed \add be\add* the Lord God of Israel from everlasting to everlasting.
\q0 So be it, so be it. 
\c 41 
\d
\v 1 For the end. For instruction. Of the sons of Korah. 
\q1
\v 2 As the deer earnestly desires the fountains of water, 
\q2 so my soul earnestly longs for you, O God. 
\q1
\v 3 My soul has thirsted for the living God: 
\q2 when will I come and appear before God? 
\q1
\v 4 My tears have been my bread day and night, 
\q2 while daily they said to me, Where is your God? 
\q1
\v 5 I remembered these things, and poured out my soul in me, 
\q2 for I will go to the place of your wondrous tabernacle, to the house of God, 
\q2 with a voice of exultation and thanksgiving 
\q2 and of the sound of those who keep festival. 
\q1
\v 6 Why are you deeply grieved, O my soul? 
\q2 And why do you trouble me? 
\q2 Hope in God; for I will give thanks to him, 
\q2 the salvation of my face, and my God. 
\q1
\v 7 My soul was troubled within me: 
\q2 therefore I will remember you from the land of Jordan, 
\q2 and of the Hermonites, from the little mountain. 
\q1
\v 8 Deep calls to deep at the voice of your cataracts: 
\q2 all your billows and your waves have gone over me. 
\q1
\v 9 By day the Lord will command his mercy, 
\q2 and by night his song shall be with me: 
\q2 a prayer to the God of my life. 
\q1
\v 10 I will say to God, You are my helper; 
\q2 why have you forgotten me? 
\q2 Why do I go sad of face, 
\q2 while \f + \fr 41:10 \ft So the Greek. The context, however, suggests the opposite reading, "while my enemy oppresses," and this is how Brenton translates it.\f*oppressing my enemy? 
\q1
\v 11 While my bones were breaking, 
\q2 those who afflicted me reproached me; 
\q2 while they said to me daily, Where is your God? 
\q1
\v 12 Why are you deeply grieved, O my soul? 
\q2 And why do you trouble me? 
\q2 Hope in God; for I will give thanks to him, 
\q2 the salvation of my face, my God. 
\c 42 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David.
\q1
\v 1b Judge me, O God, and plead my cause, against an ungodly nation: 
\q2 deliver me from the unjust and deceitful man. 
\q1
\v 2 For you, O God, are my strength: why have you cast me off? 
\q2 And why do I go sad of face, while \f + \fr 42:2 \ft So the Greek. The context, however, suggests the opposite reading, "while my enemy oppresses," and this is how Brenton translates it.\f*oppressing my enemy?
\q1
\v 3 Send forth your light and your truth: 
\q2 they have led me, 
\q2 and brought me to your holy mountain, 
\q2 and to your tents. 
\q1
\v 4 I will go in to the altar of God, 
\q2 to God who gladdens my youth: 
\q2 I will give thanks to you on the harp, O God, my God. 
\q1
\v 5 Why are you deeply grieved, O my soul? 
\q2 And why do you trouble me? 
\q2 Hope in God; for I will give thanks to him, 
\q2 the salvation of my face, my God. 
\c 43 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of the sons of Korah. A Psalm for understanding.
\q1
\v 2 O God, we have heard with our ears, 
\q2 our fathers have told us, the work which you wrought in their days, 
\q2 in the days of old. 
\q1
\v 3 Your hand completely destroyed the nations, 
\q2 and you planted them: 
\q2 you afflicted the nations, and cast them out. 
\q1
\v 4 For they didn't inherit the land by their sword, 
\q2 and their arm did not deliver them; 
\q2 but your right hand, and your arm, and the light of your face, 
\q2 because you were well pleased in them. 
\q1
\v 5 You are indeed my King and my God, 
\q2 who commands deliverances for Jacob. 
\q1
\v 6 In you will we gore our enemies, 
\q2 and in your name will we reduce to nothing those who rise up against us. 
\q1
\v 7 For I will not trust in my bow, 
\q2 and my sword will not save me. 
\q1
\v 8 For you have saved us from those who afflicted us, 
\q2 and have put to shame those who hated us. 
\q1
\v 9 In God we will be praised all the day, 
\q2 and to your name will we give thanks forever. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 10 But now you have us cast off, and put us to shame; 
\q2 and you will not go forth with our armies. 
\q1
\v 11 You have turned us back before our enemies; 
\q2 and those who hated us plundered us for themselves. 
\q1
\v 12 You made us as sheep for food; 
\q2 and you scattered us among the nations. 
\q1
\v 13 You have sold your people without price, 
\q2 and gained no profit from their sale.
\q1
\v 14 You have made us a reproach to our neighbours, 
\q2 a scorn and derision to those who are all around us. 
\q1
\v 15 You have made us a proverb among the nations, 
\q2 a shaking of the head among the nations. 
\q1
\v 16 All day my shame is before me, 
\q2 and the confusion of my face has covered me, 
\q1
\v 17 because of the voice of the slanderer and reviler; 
\q2 because of the enemy and avenger. 
\q1
\v 18 All these things have come upon us: 
\q2 but we have not forgotten you, 
\q2 neither have we dealt unrighteously in your covenant. 
\q1
\v 19 Our heart has not gone back; 
\q2 but you have turned aside our paths from your way. 
\q1
\v 20 For you have laid us low in a place of affliction, 
\q2 and the shadow of death has covered us. 
\q1
\v 21 If we have forgotten the name of our God, 
\q2 and if we have spread out our hands to a foreign god; 
\q2 will not God search these things out? 
\q1
\v 22 for he knows the secrets of the heart. 
\q1
\v 23 \x + \xo 43:23 \xt Rom. 8. 36.\x*Therefore, for your sake we are killed all day long; 
\q2 we are counted as sheep for slaughter. 
\q1
\v 24 Awake! Why do you sleep, O Lord? 
\q2 Arise, and do not cast \add us\add* off forever. 
\q1
\v 25 Why do you turn your face away, 
\q2 and forget our poverty and our affliction? 
\q1
\v 26 For our soul has been brought down to the dust; 
\q2 our belly has cleaved to the earth. 
\q1
\v 27 Arise, O Lord, help us, 
\q2 and redeem us for your name's sake. 
\c 44 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning those who will be changed. Of the sons of Korah; for instruction. A song concerning the beloved. 
\q1
\v 2 My heart has poured forth a good word: 
\q2 I declare my works to the king: 
\q2 my tongue is the pen of a swift writer. 
\q1
\v 3 You are more beautiful than the sons of men: 
\q2 grace has been shed forth on your lips: 
\q2 therefore God has blessed you forever. 
\q1
\v 4 Gird your sword upon your thigh, O Mighty One, 
\q2 in your comeliness, and in your beauty; 
\q1
\v 5 and string \add your bow\add*, and prosper, and reign, 
\q2 because of truth and meekness and righteousness; 
\q2 and your right hand will guide you wonderfully. 
\q1
\v 6 Your arrows are sharpened, Mighty One, 
\q2 the peoples will fall beneath you, 
\q2 in the heart of the king's enemies. 
\q1
\v 7 \x + \xo 44:7 \xt Heb. 1. 8-10.\x*Your throne, O God, is forever and ever: 
\q2 the sceptre of your kingdom is a sceptre of righteousness. 
\q1
\v 8 You have loved righteousness, and hated wickedness: 
\q2 therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness beyond your fellows. 
\q1
\v 9 Myrrh, and stacte, and cassia from your garments, 
\q2 out of the ivory palaces, from which they have gladdened you.
\q1
\v 10 Daughters of kings are among your honoured women;
\q2 the queen stood at your right hand, 
\q2 clothed in gold-woven garments,
\q2 adorned in varied colours. 
\q1
\v 11 Hear, O daughter, and see, and incline your ear; 
\q2 and forget your people, and your father's house. 
\q1
\v 12 Because the king has desired your beauty; 
\q2 for he is your Lord. 
\q1
\v 13 The daughters of Tyre will adore him with gifts; 
\q2 the rich of the people will entreat your \f + \fr 44:13 \fqa Gr. \ft face.\f*favour. 
\q1
\v 14 All her glory of the king's daughter is within,
\q2 clothed in golden fringed garments, embroidered.
\q1
\v 15 Virgins will be brought to the king after her: 
\q2 her companions will be brought to you. 
\q1
\v 16 They will be brought with gladness and exultation: 
\q2 they will be led into the king's temple. 
\q1
\v 17 Instead of your fathers, sons have been born to you: 
\q2 you will make them princes over all the earth. 
\q1
\v 18 They will remember your name from generation to generation: 
\q2 therefore peoples will give thanks to you forever, and forever and ever. 
\c 45 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of the sons of Korah. A psalm concerning secret things. 
\q1
\v 2 God is our refuge and strength, 
\q2 a help in the afflictions that have come heavily upon us. 
\q1
\v 3 Therefore will we not fear when the earth is troubled, 
\q2 and the mountains are removed into the depths of the seas. 
\q1
\v 4 Their waters have roared and been troubled, 
\q2 the mountains have been troubled by his might. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 The river's streams gladden the city of God: 
\q2 the Most High has sanctified his tabernacle. 
\q1
\v 6 God is among her; she will not be moved: 
\q2 God will help her at daybreak.
\q1
\v 7 The nations were troubled, the kingdoms tottered: 
\q2 he uttered his voice, the earth shook. 
\q1
\v 8 The Lord of hosts is with us; 
\q2 the God of Jacob is our helper. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 Come, and behold the works of the Lord, 
\q2 what wonders he has achieved on the earth. 
\q1
\v 10 Putting an end to wars as far as the ends of the earth; 
\q2 he will crush the bow, and break in pieces the weapon, 
\q2 and burn the bucklers with fire. 
\q1
\v 11 Be still, and know that I am God: 
\q2 I will be exalted among the nations, 
\q2 I will be exalted in the earth. 
\q1
\v 12 The Lord of hosts is with us; 
\q2 the God of Jacob is our helper. 
\c 46 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A psalm of the sons of Korah. 
\q1
\v 2 Clap your hands, all you nations; 
\q2 shout to God with a voice of exultation. 
\q1
\v 3 For the Lord most high is fearsome, 
\q2 a great king over all the earth. 
\q1
\v 4 He has subdued the peoples under us, 
\q2 and the nations under our feet. 
\q1
\v 5 He chose his inheritance for us, 
\q2 the beauty of Jacob which he loved. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 God went up with a shout, 
\q2 the Lord with a sound of a trumpet. 
\q1
\v 7 Sing praises to our God, sing praises: 
\q2 sing praises to our King, sing praises. 
\q1
\v 8 For God is king of all the earth: 
\q2 sing praises with understanding. 
\q1
\v 9 God reigns over the nations: 
\q2 God sits on the throne of his holiness. 
\q1
\v 10 The rulers of the people are assembled with the God of Abraham: 
\q2 for God's mighty ones of the earth have been greatly exalted. 
\c 47 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm. A song of the sons of Korah; on the second \add day\add* of the week. 
\q1
\v 2 Great is the Lord, 
\q2 and greatly to be praised in the city of our God, 
\q2 in his holy mountain. 
\q1
\v 3 Well-rooted, the joy of the whole earth, 
\q2 the mountains of Zion, the sides of the north,
\q2 the city of the great king.
\q1
\v 4 God is known in her palaces, 
\q2 when he undertakes to help her. 
\q1
\v 5 For, behold, the kings of the earth were assembled, 
\q2 they came together. 
\q1
\v 6 They saw, and so they wondered: 
\q2 they were troubled, they were moved. 
\q1
\v 7 Trembling took hold on them: 
\q2 there were the pangs as of a woman in labor. 
\q1
\v 8 You will break the ships of Tharsis 
\q2 with a vehement wind. 
\q1
\v 9 As we have heard, so have we also seen, 
\q2 in the city of the Lord of hosts, 
\q2 in the city of our God: 
\q2 God has founded it forever. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 10 We have thought of your mercy, O God, 
\q2 among your people. 
\q1
\v 11 According to your name, O God, 
\q2 so also is your praise to the ends of the earth: 
\q2 your right hand is full of righteousness. 
\q1
\v 12 Let mount Zion rejoice, 
\q2 let the daughters of Judæa exult, 
\q2 because of your judgments, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 13 Go around Zion, and encompass her: 
\q2 describe her towers. 
\q1
\v 14 Mark well her strength, 
\q2 and observe her palaces; 
\q2 that you may tell the next generation. 
\q1
\v 15 For this is our God forever and ever: 
\q2 he will be our guide for evermore. 
\c 48 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Psalm of the sons of Korah. 
\q1
\v 2 Hear these words, all you nations, 
\q2 listen, all you who dwell upon the earth: 
\q1
\v 3 both the earth-born and the sons of men; 
\q2 the rich and poor together. 
\q1
\v 4 My mouth will speak of wisdom; 
\q2 and the meditation of my heart \add will speak\add* understanding. 
\q1
\v 5 I will incline my ear to a parable: 
\q2 I will open my riddle on the harp. 
\q1
\v 6 Why should I fear in the evil day? 
\q2 The wickedness of my heel will surround me. 
\q1
\v 7 Those who trust in their strength, 
\q2 and boast themselves in the multitude of their wealth— 
\q1
\v 8 A brother does not redeem, will a man redeem? 
\q2 He will not give to God a ransom for himself, 
\q1
\v 9 nor the price of the redemption of his soul. 
\q2 It has ceased forever; 
\q1
\v 10 and shall he live forever,
\q2 so as not to see corruption 
\q1
\v 11 when he sees wise men dying? 
\q2 The fool and the senseless one will perish together; 
\q2 and they will leave their wealth to strangers. 
\q1
\v 12 Their tombs are their houses forever, 
\q2 their tents to all generations: 
\q2 they have called their lands after their own names. 
\q1
\v 13 Man being in honour, doesn't understand: 
\q2 he is compared to senseless beasts, and is like them. 
\q1
\v 14 This their way is an offence to them: 
\q2 yet afterwards men will commend their sayings. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 15 They were placed like sheep in Hades; 
\q2 death shepherds them; 
\q2 and the upright will have dominion over them in the morning, 
\q2 and their help will decay in Hades, away from their glory. 
\q1
\v 16 But God will deliver my soul from the hand of Hades, 
\q2 when he receives me. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 17 Don't be afraid when a man is enriched, 
\q2 and when the glory of his house is increased. 
\q1
\v 18 For he will take nothing when he dies; 
\q2 neither will his glory descend with him. 
\q1
\v 19 For his soul will be blessed in his life: 
\q2 he will give thanks to you when you do well to him. 
\q1
\v 20 \add Yet\add* he will go to the generation of his fathers; 
\q2 he will never see light. 
\q1
\v 21 Man being in honour, doesn't understand: 
\q2 he is compared to senseless beasts, and is like them. 
\c 49 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 1b The God of gods, the Lord, has spoken, 
\q2 and called the earth from the rising of the sun to its going down.
\q1
\v 2 Out of Zion, the excellence of his beauty,
\q2 God will come manifestly, 
\q1
\v 3 our God, and will not keep silence. 
\q2 A fire will be kindled before him, 
\q2 and all around him there will be a very great tempest. 
\q1
\v 4 He will summon the heaven above, and the earth, 
\q2 that he may \x + \xo 49:4 \xt Heb. 10. 30.\x*judge his people. 
\q1
\v 5 Assemble his saints to him, 
\q2 those who have made a covenant with him upon sacrifices. 
\q1
\v 6 The heavens will declare his righteousness: 
\q2 for God is judge. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 Hear, my people, and I will speak to you, O Israel: 
\q2 and I will testify to you: I am God, your God. 
\q1
\v 8 I will not reprove you on account of your sacrifices; 
\q2 for your whole-burnt-offerings are before me continually. 
\q1
\v 9 I will take no bullocks out of your house, 
\q2 nor he-goats out of your flocks. 
\q1
\v 10 For all the wild beasts of the thicket are mine, 
\q2 the livestock on the mountains, and oxen. 
\q1
\v 11 I know all the birds of the sky; 
\q2 and the beauty of the field is with me. 
\q1
\v 12 If I should be hungry, I will not tell you: 
\q2 for the world is mine, and all that fills it.
\q1
\v 13 Will I eat the flesh of bulls, 
\q2 or drink the blood of goats? 
\q1
\v 14 Offer to God the sacrifice of praise; 
\q2 and pay your vows to the Most High. 
\q1
\v 15 Call upon me in the day of affliction; 
\q2 and I will deliver you, and you will glorify me. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 16 But to the sinner God has said, 
\q2 Why do you declare my ordinances, 
\q2 and take up my covenant in your mouth? 
\q1
\v 17 Because you have hated instruction, 
\q2 and have cast my words behind \add you\add*. 
\q1
\v 18 If you saw a thief, you \f + \fr 49:18 \ft See 1 Pe 4. 4.\f*ran along with him, 
\q2 and have cast in your lot with adulterers. 
\q1
\v 19 Your mouth has multiplied wickedness, 
\q2 and your tongue has framed deceit.\f + \fr 49:19 \ft From these words in \fqa Alex. \ft to \xt Ps. 79. 11, \ft thirty psalms are missing.\f* 
\q1
\v 20 You sat and spoke against your brother, 
\q2 and scandalised your mother's son. 
\q1
\v 21 These things you did, and I kept silence: 
\q2 you wickedly supposed that I should be like you. 
\q2 I will reprove you, and set \add them\add* before you. 
\q1
\v 22 Now consider these things, you who forget God, 
\q2 lest he tear \add you\add*, and there be no deliverer. 
\q1
\v 23 The sacrifice of praise will glorify me: 
\q2 and in that way I will show him the salvation of God. 
\c 50 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Psalm of David,
\d
\v 2 when Nathan the prophet came to him, when he had gone in to Beersheba. 
\q1
\v 3 Have mercy upon me, O God, according to your great mercy; 
\q2 and according to the multitude of your compassions blot out my transgression. 
\q1
\v 4 Wash me thoroughly from my wickedness, 
\q2 and cleanse me from my sin. 
\q1
\v 5 For I am conscious of my wickedness; 
\q2 and my sin is continually before me. 
\q1
\v 6 Against you only have I sinned, and done evil before you: 
\q2 that you might be justified in your sayings, and might overcome when you are judged. 
\q1
\v 7 For, behold, I was conceived in iniquities, 
\q2 and in sins did my mother conceive me. 
\q1
\v 8 For, behold, you love truth: 
\q2 you have manifested to me the secret and hidden things of your wisdom. 
\q1
\v 9 You will sprinkle me with hyssop, and I will be purified: 
\q2 you will wash me, and I will be made whiter than snow. 
\q1
\v 10 You will cause me to hear gladness and joy: 
\q2 the afflicted bones will rejoice. 
\q1
\v 11 Turn away your face from my sins, 
\q2 and blot out all my iniquities. 
\q1
\v 12 Create in me a clean heart, O God; 
\q2 and renew a right spirit in my inward parts. 
\q1
\v 13 Don't cast me away from your presence; 
\q2 and don't remove your Holy Spirit from me. 
\q1
\v 14 Restore to me the joy of your salvation: 
\q2 establish me with your \f + \fr 50:14 \fqa Gr. \ft governing.\f*directing Spirit. 
\q1
\v 15 I will teach transgressors your ways; 
\q2 and ungodly men will turn to you. 
\q1
\v 16 Deliver me from blood-guiltiness, O God, the God of my salvation: 
\q2 my tongue will joyfully declare your righteousness. 
\q1
\v 17 O Lord, you will open my lips; 
\q2 and my mouth will declare your praise. 
\q1
\v 18 For if you desired sacrifice, I would have given \add it\add*: 
\q2 you will not take pleasure in whole-burnt-offerings. 
\q1
\v 19 Sacrifice to God is a broken spirit: 
\q2 a broken and humbled heart God will not despise. 
\q1
\v 20 Do good, O Lord, to Zion in your good pleasure; 
\q2 and let the walls of Jerusalem be built. 
\q1
\v 21 Then you will be pleased with a sacrifice of righteousness, \f + \fr 50:21 \ft Properly, a heave or wave-offering.\f*offering, and whole-burnt-sacrifices: 
\q2 then will they offer calves upon your altar. 
\c 51 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of instruction. Of David, 
\d
\v 2 when Doeg the Idumean came and told Saul, and said to him, David has gone to the house of Abimelech. 
\q1
\v 3 Why do you boast in evil, O mighty one?,
\q2 \add You practice\add* lawlessness all the day.
\q1
\v 4 Your tongue has devised injustice;
\q2 like a sharpened razor, you have worked deceit. 
\q1
\v 5 You have loved wickedness more than goodness; 
\q2 unrighteousness better than to speak righteousness. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 You have loved all words of destruction, 
\q2 \add and\add* a deceitful tongue. 
\q1
\v 7 Therefore may God destroy you forever, 
\q2 may he pluck you up and remove you from \add your\add* dwelling, 
\q2 and your root from the land of the living. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 8 The righteous will see, and fear, 
\q2 and will laugh at him, and say, 
\q1
\v 9 Behold the man who didn't make God his help; 
\q2 but trusted in the abundance of his wealth, 
\q2 and strengthened himself in his vanity. 
\q1
\v 10 But I am as a fruitful olive in the house of God: 
\q2 I have trusted in the mercy of God forever, 
\q2 even forever and ever. 
\q1
\v 11 I will give thanks to you forever, for you have done \add it\add*: 
\q2 and I will wait on your name; 
\q2 for \add it is\add* good before your saints. 
\c 52 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Upon Maeleth. Of instruction. Of David.
\q1
\v 2 The fool has said in his heart, There is no God. 
\q2 They were corrupted and became abominable in iniquities: 
\q2 there is no one who does good. 
\q1
\v 3 God looked down from heaven upon the sons of men,
\q2 to see if there were any who understood, or sought after God. 
\q1
\v 4 \x + \xo 52:4 \xt Rom. 3. 10-12.\x*They have all turned aside,
\q2 together they have become unprofitable; 
\q2 there is no one who does good, there isn't even one. 
\q1
\v 5 Will not all the workers of wickedness know, 
\q2 who devour my people as they would eat bread? 
\q1 They have not called upon God. 
\q2 There they will be greatly afraid, where there was no fear: 
\q1
\v 6 for God has scattered the bones of the men-pleasers; 
\q2 they were ashamed, for God despised them. 
\q1
\v 7 Who will bring the salvation of Israel out of Zion? 
\q2 When the Lord turns the captivity of his people, 
\q2 Jacob will exult, and Israel will be glad. 
\c 53 
\d
\v 1 For the end. With Hymns. Of instruction. Of David, 
\d
\v 2 when the Ziphites came and said to Saul, Behold, isn't David hidden with us? 
\q1
\v 3 Save me, O God, by your name, 
\q2 and judge me by your might. 
\q1
\v 4 O God, hear my prayer; 
\q2 listen to the words of my mouth. 
\q1
\v 5 For strangers have risen up against me, 
\q2 and mighty men have sought my life: 
\q2 they have not set God before them. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 For behold! God assists me; 
\q2 and the Lord is the helper of my soul. 
\q1
\v 7 He will return evil to my enemies; 
\q2 completely destroy them in your truth. 
\q1
\v 8 I will willingly sacrifice to you: 
\q2 I will give thanks to your name, O Lord; for \add it is\add* good. 
\q1
\v 9 For you have delivered me out of all affliction, 
\q2 and my eye has looked on my enemies. 
\c 54 
\d
\v 1 For the end. With Hymns. Of instruction. Of David. 
\q1
\v 2 Listen, O God, to my prayer;
\q2 and don't disregard my supplication. 
\q1
\v 3 Pay attention to me, and listen to me: 
\q2 I was grieved in my meditation, and troubled; 
\q1
\v 4 because of the voice of the enemy, 
\q2 and because of the oppression of the sinner: 
\q1 for they brought wickedness against me, 
\q2 and in anger they bore a grudge against me.
\q1
\v 5 My heart was troubled within me; 
\q2 and the fear of death fell upon me. 
\q1
\v 6 Fear and trembling came upon me, 
\q2 and darkness covered me. 
\q1
\v 7 I said, O that I had wings as of a dove! 
\q2 Then would I fly away, and be at rest. 
\q1
\v 8 Behold! I have fled far off, 
\q2 and lodged in the wilderness. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 I waited for him who saves me from distress of spirit and tempest. 
\q1
\v 10 Overwhelm, O Lord, and divide their tongues: 
\q2 for I have seen wickedness and strife in the city. 
\q1
\v 11 Day and night wickedness circles her on her walls, 
\q2 and trouble is in her midst, and unrighteousness;
\q1
\v 12 and usury and deceit have not failed from its streets. 
\q1
\v 13 For if an enemy had reproached me, I would have endured it; 
\q2 and if one who hated had spoken vauntingly against me, I would have hidden myself from him. 
\q1
\v 14 But you, O kindred spirit, 
\q2 my guide, and my companion, 
\q1
\v 15 who in companionship with me sweetened \add our\add* food: 
\q2 we walked in the house of God in harmony. 
\q1
\v 16 Let death come upon them, 
\q2 and let them go down alive into Hades, 
\q2 for wickedness is in their dwellings, in their midst. 
\q1
\v 17 I cried to God, and the Lord listened to me. 
\q1
\v 18 Evening, and morning, and at noon I will declare and make known:
\q2 and he will hear my voice. 
\q1
\v 19 He will deliver my soul in peace from those who draw near to me: 
\q2 for they were with me among many.
\q1
\v 20 God will hear, and bring them low, 
\q2 he who has exists from eternity. \qs Pause.\qs*
\q1 For there is no change for them,
\q2 and they have not feared God. 
\q1
\v 21 He has reached forth his hand for retribution; 
\q2 they have profaned his covenant. 
\q1
\v 22 They were scattered at the anger of his face, 
\q2 and his heart drew near; 
\q2 his words were smoother than oil,
\q2 yet are they darts. 
\q1
\v 23 \x + \xo 54:23 \xt 1 Pet. 5. 7.\x*Cast your care upon the Lord, and he will \f + \fr 54:23 \fqa Or, \ft nourish.\f*sustain you; 
\q2 he will never allow the righteous to be shaken. 
\q1
\v 24 But you, O God, will bring them down to the pit of destruction; 
\q2 bloody and deceitful men will not live out half their days; 
\q2 but I will hope in you, O Lord. 
\c 55 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning the people that were removed from the holy \add things\add*. Of David, for a memorial, when the Philistines seized him in Gath. 
\q1
\v 2 Have mercy upon me, O God;
\q2 for a man has trodden me down; 
\q2 all the day long, warring against me, he has afflicted me. 
\q1
\v 3 \f + \fr 55:3--4 \ft Brenton's translation of verses 3 and 4 is based on Greek text that has slight differences from the Rahlfs Greek used as a reference here. He has: (3) Mine enemies have trodden me down all the day from the dawning of the day; for there are many warring against me. (4) They shall be afraid, but I will trust in thee.\f*My enemies have trodden me down all the day long;
\q2 for many are those warring against me from on high. 
\q1
\v 4 By day I will be afraid, 
\q2 but I will trust in you. 
\q1
\v 5 In God I will praise my words; 
\q2 all day long I have hoped in God; 
\q2 I will not fear. What will \add mere\add* flesh do to me?
\q1
\v 6 All day long they have abominated my words; 
\q2 all their schemes are against me for evil. 
\q1
\v 7 They will dwell near and hide;
\q2 they will watch my steps,
\q2 just as they have lain in wait for my soul. 
\q1
\v 8 For nothing will you save them; 
\q2 you will bring down the peoples in wrath. 
\q1
\v 9 O God, I have declared my life to you; 
\q2 you have set my tears before you, 
\q2 according to your promise. 
\q1
\v 10 My enemies will be turned back, 
\q2 in the day when I call on you; 
\q2 behold, I know that you are my God. 
\q1
\v 11 In God I will praise \add his\add* word; 
\q2 in the Lord I will praise \add his\add* saying. 
\q1
\v 12 In God I have hoped;
\q2 I will not be afraid. What will \add mere\add* man do to me? 
\q1
\v 13 O God, vows are upon me which I will pay: praise to you, 
\q1
\v 14 for you have delivered my soul from death, 
\q2 and my feet from sliding, 
\q2 that I should be pleasing before God in the land of the living. 
\c 56 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Don't destroy. Of David, for a memorial, when he fled from the presence of Saul to the cave. 
\q1
\v 2 Have mercy, upon me, O God, have mercy upon me: 
\q2 for my soul has trusted in you: 
\q2 and in the shadow of your wings will I hope, 
\q2 until the wickedness have passed away. 
\q1
\v 3 I will cry to God most high; 
\q2 the God who has benefited me. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 He sent from heaven and saved me; 
\q2 he gave to reproach those who trampled on me: 
\q2 God has sent his mercy and his truth; 
\q1
\v 5 and he has delivered my soul from the midst of young lions:
\q2 I lay down to sleep, \add though\add* troubled. 
\q2 \add As for\add* the sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows,
\q2 and their tongue a sharp sword. 
\q1
\v 6 Be exalted, O God, above the heavens; 
\q2 and your glory above all the earth. 
\q1
\v 7 They have prepared snares for my feet, 
\q2 and have bowed down my soul: 
\q2 they have dug a pit before my face, 
\q2 and fallen into it \add themselves\add*. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 8 My heart, O God, \add is\add* ready, 
\q2 my heart \add is\add* ready: 
\q2 I will sing, yes will sing psalms. 
\q1
\v 9 Awake, my glory; 
\q2 awake, psaltery and harp: 
\q2 I will awake early. 
\q1
\v 10 O Lord, I will give thanks to you among the nations: 
\q2 I will sing to you among the nations. 
\q1
\v 11 For your mercy has been magnified even to the heavens, 
\q2 and your truth to the clouds. 
\q1
\v 12 Be exalted, O God, above the heavens; 
\q2 and your glory above all the earth. 
\c 57 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Don't destroy. Of David, for a memorial. 
\q1
\v 2 Do you really speak righteousness?
\q2 Do you judge uprightly, O sons of men? 
\q1
\v 3 For you work iniquities in your hearts in the earth: 
\q2 your hands plot unrighteousness. 
\q1
\v 4 Sinners have become estranged from the womb: 
\q2 they have gone astray from the belly: 
\q2 they speak lies. 
\q1
\v 5 Their venom is like that of a snake; 
\q2 like that of a deaf asp that blocks its ears; 
\q1
\v 6 which will not hear the voice of charmers, 
\q2 nor \add heed\add* a charm prepared skilfully by a wise man. 
\q1
\v 7 God has crushed their teeth in their mouth: 
\q2 the Lord has broken the molars of the lions. 
\q1
\v 8 They will be brought to nothing like water flowing away:
\q2 he will bend his bow till they fail. 
\q1
\v 9 They will be destroyed like melted wax: 
\q2 fire has fallen, and they have not seen the sun. 
\q1
\v 10 Before your thorns feel the bramble, 
\q2 as living men,
\q2 as in wrath,
\q3 he will swallow you up.
\q1
\v 11 The righteous will rejoice when he sees the vengeance upon the ungodly: 
\q2 he will wash his hands in the blood of the sinner. 
\q1
\v 12 A man will say, Truly then there is \f + \fr 57:12 \fqa Gr. \ft fruit.\f*a reward for the righteous: 
\q2 truly there is a God who judges them in the earth. 
\c 58 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Don't destroy. Of David, for a memorial, when Saul sent, and watched his house to kill him. 
\q1
\v 2 Deliver me from my enemies, O God; 
\q2 and ransom me from those who rise up against me. 
\q1
\v 3 Deliver me from the workers of wickedness, 
\q2 and save me from men of blood. 
\q1
\v 4 For, behold, they have hunted my soul; 
\q2 violent men have set upon me: 
\q2 neither \add for\add* my wickedness, nor \add for\add* my sin, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 5 Without wickedness I ran and \f + \fr 58:5 \fqa Gr. \ft Some manuscripts (including that used by Brenton) read ``I directed''; Rahlfs reads ``they directed''.\f*directed my way: 
\q2 awake to meet me, and behold. 
\q1
\v 6 You, Lord God of hosts, the God of Israel,
\q2 draw near to visit all the nations. 
\q2 Don't pity any who work wickedness. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 They will return at evening, 
\q2 and hunger like a dog, 
\q2 and go all around the city. 
\q1
\v 8 Behold, they pour forth with their mouth, 
\q2 and a sword is in their lips; 
\q2 for \add they say\add*, Who has heard? 
\q1
\v 9 But you, Lord, will laugh them to scorn; 
\q2 you will completely despise all the nations. 
\q1
\v 10 O my strength, I will watch for you; 
\q2 for you, O God, are my helper. 
\q1
\v 11 My God—his mercy will go before me: 
\q2 my God will show me \add vengeance\add* on my enemies. 
\q1
\v 12 Don't kill them, lest they forget \f + \fr 58:12 \fqa Gr. \ft some MSS read νομου σου ``your law'' (including Brenton's Greek) or ονοματος σου ``your name'' instead of λαου μου ``my people''.\f*my people; 
\q2 scatter them by your power; 
\q2 and bring them down, O Lord, my defender. 
\q1
\v 13 \add For\add* the sin of their mouth, 
\q2 \add and\add* the word of their lips, 
\q2 let them be caught in their pride. 
\q1
\v 14 And because of cursing and falsehood destructions will be denounced: 
\q2 in the wrath of destruction they will cease to be. 
\q2 Then they know that God rules over Jacob
\q2 to the ends of the earth. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 15 They will return at evening, 
\q2 and be hungry as a dog, 
\q2 and go all around the city. 
\q1
\v 16 They will be scattered in search of food; 
\q2 and if they are not satisfied, 
\q2 they will also murmur. 
\q1
\v 17 But I will sing to your strength, 
\q2 and in the morning will I exult \add in\add* your mercy; 
\q2 for you have been my supporter, 
\q2 and my refuge in the day of my affliction. 
\q1
\v 18 My helper, to you I will sing; 
\q2 O my God, you are my supporter: 
\q2 my God, my mercy. 
\c 59 
\d
\v 1 For the end. For those who will yet be changed; for an inscription. Of David for instruction, 
\d
\v 2 when he had burned Mesopotamia of Syria, and Syria Sobal, and Joab \add had\add* returned and struck twelve thousand \add in\add* the valley of salt. 
\q1
\v 3 O God, you have rejected and destroyed us; 
\q2 you have been angry, yet have pitied us. 
\q1
\v 4 You have shaken the earth, and troubled it; 
\q2 heal its breaches, for it has been shaken. 
\q1
\v 5 You have shown your people hard things: 
\q2 you have made us drink the wine of astonishment. 
\q1
\v 6 You have given a token to those who fear you, 
\q2 that they might flee from the bow. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 That your beloved ones may be delivered, 
\q2 save with your right hand, and hear me. 
\q1
\v 8 God has spoken in his holiness: 
\q2 I will rejoice, and divide Shechem, and measure out the valley of tents. 
\q1
\v 9 Gilead is mine, and Manasseh is mine; 
\q2 and Ephraim is the \f + \fr 59:9 \fqa Or, \ft strengthening.\f*strength of my head; 
\q1
\v 10 Judah is my king; 
\q2 Moab is the caldron of my hope; 
\q2 I will stretch out my shoe over Idumea; 
\q2 the Philistines have been subjected to me. 
\q1
\v 11 Who will lead me into the fortified city? 
\q2 Who will guide me as far as Idumea? 
\q1
\v 12 Who but you, O God, who have cast us off? 
\q2 And will you not go out with our forces, O God? 
\q1
\v 13 Give us help from trouble:
\q2 for vain is the deliverance of man. 
\q1
\v 14 In God will we \f + \fr 59:14 \fqa Gr. \ft work power.\f*do valiantly; 
\q2 and he will bring to nothing those who harass us. 
\c 60 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Among the Hymns of David. 
\q1
\v 2 O God, listen to my petition; 
\q2 pay attention to my prayer. 
\q1
\v 3 From the ends of the earth have I cried to you, 
\q2 when my heart was in trouble: 
\q2 you lifted me up on a rock, 
\q2 you guided me: 
\q1
\v 4 because you were my hope, 
\q2 a tower of strength from the face of the enemy. 
\q1
\v 5 I will dwell in your tabernacle forever; 
\q2 I will shelter myself under the shadow of your wings. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 For you, O God, have heard my prayers; 
\q2 you have given an inheritance to those who fear your name. 
\q1
\v 7 You will add days to the days of the king; 
\q2 his years to generation after generation. 
\q1
\v 8 He will endure forever before God: 
\q2 which of them will seek out his mercy and truth? 
\q1
\v 9 So will I sing to your name forever and ever, 
\q2 that I may daily perform my vows. 
\c 61 
\d
\v 1 For the end. For Idithun. A Psalm of David.
\q1
\v 2 Will not my soul be subject to God? 
\q2 For from him is my salvation. 
\q1
\v 3 For he is my God and my Saviour; 
\q2 my helper, I will not be shaken any more.
\q1
\v 4 How long will you assault a man? 
\q2 You are all killing, as a leaning wall
\q2 and a fence that has been pushed over.
\q1
\v 5 They only took counsel to cast down my honour;
\q2 they ran in falsehood;
\q2 with their mouth they blessed,
\q2 but with their heart they cursed. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 Nevertheless you, my soul, be subjected to God; 
\q2 for from him is my patient hope. 
\q1
\v 7 For he is my God and my Saviour; 
\q2 my helper, I will not be removed. 
\q1
\v 8 On God rests my salvation and my glory: 
\q2 the God of my help, and my hope rests on God. 
\q1
\v 9 Hope in him, all you congregation of the people; 
\q2 pour out your hearts before him, 
\q2 for God is our helper. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 10 But the sons of men are vain; 
\q2 the sons of men are false;
\q2 in the balances they deal unjustly;
\q2 they are altogether vanity. 
\q1
\v 11 Don't trust in unrighteousness, 
\q2 and don't lust after plunder: 
\q2 if wealth should flow in, 
\q2 don't set your heart upon it. 
\q1
\v 12 God has spoken once, 
\q2 \add and\add* I have heard these two things, 
\q2 that power is of God; 
\q1
\v 13 and mercy is yours, O Lord; 
\q2 for you will recompense every one according to his works. 
\c 62 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David, when he was in the wilderness of Judea. 
\q1
\v 2 O God, my God, I seek you early; 
\q2 my soul has thirsted for you: 
\q2 how greatly has my flesh \add longed\add* for you, 
\q2 in a barren and trackless and waterless land! 
\q1
\v 3 Thus have I appeared before you in the sanctuary, 
\q2 that I might see your power and your glory. 
\q1
\v 4 For your mercy is better than \f + \fr 62:4 \fqa Gr. \ft lives.\f*life: 
\q2 my lips will praise you. 
\q1
\v 5 Thus I will bless you during my life: 
\q2 I will lift up my hands in your name. 
\q1
\v 6 Let my soul be filled as with marrow and fatness; 
\q2 and my mouth will praise \add you\add* with lips of rejoicing.
\q1
\v 7 \f + \fr 62:7 \fqa Gr. \ft if.\f*When I remembered you on my bed: 
\q2 in the early morning I meditated on you. 
\q1
\v 8 For you have been my helper, 
\q2 and I will rejoice in the shelter of your wings. 
\q1
\v 9 My soul has stuck close behind you: 
\q2 your right hand has upheld me. 
\q1
\v 10 But they vainly sought after my soul; 
\q2 they will go into the lowest parts of the earth. 
\q1
\v 11 They will be delivered up to the power of the sword; 
\q2 they will be portions for foxes. 
\q1
\v 12 But the king will rejoice in God; 
\q2 every one that swears by him will be praised; 
\q2 for the mouth of those who speak unjust things has been stopped. 
\c 63 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Psalm of David. 
\q1
\v 2 Hear my prayer, O God, when I make my petition to you; 
\q2 deliver my soul from fear of the enemy. 
\q1
\v 3 You have sheltered me from the conspiracy of those who do wickedly; 
\q2 from the multitude of those who work wickedness; 
\q1
\v 4 who have sharpened their tongues as a sword; 
\q2 they have bent their bow maliciously; 
\q1
\v 5 to shoot in secret at the blameless; 
\q2 they will shoot him suddenly, and will not fear. 
\q1
\v 6 They have strengthened an evil plan for themselves, 
\q2 they have plotted to hide snares; 
\q2 they have said, Who will see them? 
\q1
\v 7 They searched out wickedness; 
\q2 they wearied themselves searching diligently, 
\q2 a man will approach, and the heart is deep, 
\q1
\v 8 and God will be exalted, 
\q2 an arrow of children became their wounds,
\q1
\v 9 and their tongues became weak against them.
\q2 All who saw them were troubled; 
\q1
\v 10 and every man feared,
\q2 and they related the works of God, 
\q2 and understood his deeds. 
\q1
\v 11 The righteous will rejoice in the Lord, 
\q2 and hope on him, 
\q2 and all the upright in heart will be praised. 
\c 64 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A psalm of David. A song.\f+ \fr 64:1 \ft Rahlfs adds: “Of Jeremiah and Ezekiel, concerning the word of the sojourning/captivity, when they were about to depart.” This addition is absent from Vaticanus and Sinaiticus manuscripts.\f*
\q1
\v 2 Praise becomes you, O God, in Zion; 
\q2 and to you will the vow be performed. 
\q1
\v 3 Hear my prayer; 
\q2 to you all flesh will come. 
\q1
\v 4 The words of lawlessness have overpowered us; 
\q2 but you will make atonement for our ungodliness. 
\q1
\v 5 Blessed is he whom you have chosen and adopted; 
\q2 he will dwell in your courts; 
\q2 we will be filled with the good things of your house; 
\q2 your temple is holy. 
\q1
\v 6 \add You are\add* wonderful in righteousness. 
\q2 Hear us, O God our Saviour; 
\q2 the hope of all the ends of the earth, 
\q2 and of those far away upon the sea:
\q1
\v 7 establishing the mountains by his strength, 
\q2 being girded about with power; 
\q1
\v 8 who troubles the depths of the sea, 
\q2 the sounds of its waves. 
\q1
\v 9 The nations will be troubled, 
\q2 and those who inhabit the ends \add of the earth\add* will be afraid of your signs; 
\q2 you will make the outgoings of morning and evening rejoice. 
\q1
\v 10 You have visited the earth, and saturated it; 
\q2 you have abundantly enriched it. 
\q2 The river of God is filled with water; 
\q2 you have prepared their food, 
\q2 for thus is your preparation.
\q1
\v 11 Saturate \f + \fr 64:11 \ft In Greek, "the earth" is a feminine noun, hence the feminine pronouns here.\f*her furrows, multiply her produce; 
\q2 she will rejoice in her drops as she springs up.
\q1
\v 12 You will bless the crown of the year \add because\add* of your goodness; 
\q2 and your plains will be filled with abundance. 
\q1
\v 13 The mountains of the wilderness will be enriched; 
\q2 and the hills will gird themselves with joy. 
\q1
\v 14 The rams of the flock have clothed themselves,
\q2 and the valleys will abound in grain; 
\q1 they will cry aloud,
\q2 yes, they will sing hymns. 
\c 65 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Song of a Psalm \f + \fr 65:1 \ft Rahlfs brackets [ἀναστάσεως] "of resurrection" and after noting that it is absent in the Siniaticus MS, adds that "it is a Christian addition showing that this Psalm was already being sung on the feast of the Resurrection in the 2nd or 1st century in whose Mass even now four of its stichoi are sung (1:2¹, 3¹, 4¹ up to column 10)" and he cites the source for that liturgical evidence: a printed Pentecostarion (the Orthodox liturgical book used from Easter to Pentecost).\f*[of resurrection]. 
\q1
\v 1b Shout to God, all the earth. 
\q1
\v 2 O sing praises to his name; 
\q2 give glory to his praise. 
\q1
\v 3 Say to God, How awesome are your works! 
\q2 Through the greatness of your power your enemies will act falsely to you. 
\q1
\v 4 Let all the earth worship you, and sing to you; 
\q2 let them sing to your name. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 Come and behold the works of God; 
\q2 he is awesome in counsels beyond the children of men. 
\q1
\v 6 Who turns the sea into dry land; 
\q2 they will go through the river on foot; 
\q2 there we will rejoice in him, 
\q1
\v 7 who has dominion by his power for ever,
\q2 his eyes look upon the nations; 
\q2 don't let those who provoke \add him\add* be exalted in themselves. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 8 Bless our God, you nations, 
\q2 and make the voice of his praise be heard; 
\q1
\v 9 who quickens my soul in life, 
\q2 and does not allow my feet to be shaken. 
\q1
\v 10 For you, O God, have proved us; 
\q2 you have tried us with fire as silver is tried. 
\q1
\v 11 You brought us into the snare; 
\q2 you laid afflictions on our back. 
\q1
\v 12 You set men over our heads; 
\q2 we went through fire and water; 
\q2 but you brought us out into renewal. 
\q1
\v 13 I will go into your house with whole-burnt-offerings; 
\q2 I will pay you my vows, 
\q1
\v 14 which my lips framed, 
\q2 and my mouth uttered in my affliction. 
\q1
\v 15 I will offer to you whole-burnt-sacrifices full of marrow, 
\q2 with incense and rams; 
\q2 I will sacrifice to you oxen with goats. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 16 Come, hear, and I will tell, 
\q2 all you who fear God, 
\q2 how great things he has done for my soul. 
\q1
\v 17 I cried to him with my mouth, 
\q2 and exalted him with my tongue. 
\q1
\v 18 If I have regarded wickedness in my heart, 
\q2 don't let the Lord listen. 
\q1
\v 19 Therefore God has listened to me; 
\q2 he has attended to the voice of my prayer. 
\q1
\v 20 Blessed be God, 
\q2 who has not turned away my prayer, 
\q2 nor his mercy from me. 
\c 66 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Among the hymns. A psalm \f + \fr 66:1 \ft The Vaticanus MS has ``of David''.\f*of a song.
\q1
\v 2 God be merciful to us, and bless us; 
\q2 cause his face to shine upon us. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 3 That your way may be known on the earth, 
\q2 your salvation among all nations. 
\q1
\v 4 Let the peoples, O God, give thanks to you; 
\q2 let all the peoples give thanks to you. 
\q1
\v 5 Let the nations rejoice and exult, 
\q2 for you will judge the peoples in equity, 
\q2 and will guide the nations on the earth. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 Let the peoples, O God, give thanks to you; 
\q2 let all the peoples give thanks to you. 
\q1
\v 7 The earth has yielded her fruit; 
\q2 let God, our God, bless us. 
\q1
\v 8 Let God bless us; 
\q2 and let all the ends of the earth fear him. 
\c 67 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of David. A psalm of a song.
\q1
\v 2 Let God arise, and let his enemies be scattered; 
\q2 and let those who hate him flee from before him. 
\q1
\v 3 As smoke vanishes,
\q2 let them vanish: 
\q1 as wax melts before the fire,
\q2 so let the sinners perish from before God. 
\q1
\v 4 But let the righteous rejoice; 
\q2 let them exult before God: 
\q2 let them be delighted with joy. 
\q1
\v 5 Sing to God, sing praises to his name: 
\q2 make a way for him who rides upon the west 
\q2 (the Lord is his name) and exult before him. 
\q2 They will be troubled before his face,
\q1
\v 6 the father of the orphans, 
\q2 and defender of the widows: 
\q2 God in his holy place. 
\q1
\v 7 God settles the solitary in a house; 
\q2 leading forth prisoners mightily, 
\q2 likewise those who act provokingly, 
\q2 who dwell in tombs. 
\q1
\v 8 O God, when you went forth before your people, 
\q2 when you went through the wilderness; Pause: 
\q1
\v 9 the earth quaked, 
\q2 yes, the heavens dripped at the presence of the God of Sinai, 
\q2 at the presence of the God of Israel. 
\q1
\v 10 O God, you will grant to your inheritance a gracious rain; 
\q2 for it was weary, but you refreshed it. 
\q1
\v 11 Your creatures dwell in it: 
\q2 you have in your goodness prepared for the poor. 
\q1
\v 12 The Lord God will give a word 
\q2 to those who preach in a great company. 
\q1
\v 13 \f + \fr 67:13–15 \ft The meaning of the Greek text of these verses is obscure.\f*The king of the forces of the beloved, 
\q2 even for the beauty of the house
\q2 to divide the spoils. 
\q1
\v 14 Even if you should lie among the lots, 
\q2 the wings of a dove are covered with silver, 
\q2 and her breast with the green sheen of gold. 
\q1
\v 15 When the heavenly One scatters kings upon it, 
\q2 they will be made snow-white in Selmon. 
\q1
\v 16 The mountain of God is a rich mountain; 
\q2 a \f + \fr 67:16 \fqa Gr. \ft curdled like cheese.\f*swelling mountain, a rich mountain. 
\q1
\v 17 Why do you imagine, you swelling mountains, 
\q2 the mountain which God has delighted to dwell in?
\q2 For indeed the Lord will dwell there forever. 
\q1
\v 18 The chariots of God are ten thousand fold, 
\q2 thousands of rejoicing ones: 
\q2 the Lord is among them, in Sinai, in the holy place. 
\q1
\v 19 \f + \fr 67:19 \fqa Gr. \ft having gone up.\f*You have gone up on high, you have led captivity captive, 
\q2 you have received gifts among men,
\q2 yes, even among the rebellious, 
\q2 that you might dwell there. 
\q1
\v 20 Blessed be the Lord God, 
\q2 blessed be the Lord daily; 
\q2 and the God of our salvation will prosper us. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 21 Our God is the God of salvation; 
\q2 and to the Lord belong the ways out of death. 
\q1
\v 22 But God will crush the heads of his enemies; 
\q2 the hairy pate of those who go on in their trespasses. 
\q1
\v 23 The Lord said, I will bring back from Bashan, 
\q2 I will bring back through the depths of the sea. 
\q1
\v 24 That your foot may be dipped in blood, 
\q2 the tongue of your dogs from the enemies. 
\q1
\v 25 Your processions, O God, have been seen; 
\q2 the processions of my God, the king, in the sanctuary. 
\q1
\v 26 The princes went first, 
\q2 followed by the singers,
\q2 among young women playing on timbrels. 
\q1
\v 27 Praise God in the congregations, 
\q2 the Lord from the fountains of Israel. 
\q1
\v 28 There is Benjamin the younger \add one\add* in \f + \fr 67:28 \fqa Gr. \ft ἐκστάσει has a broad semantic range and might mean in ecstasy, astonishment, bewilderment, terror/panic, anguish, etc. It has the idea of being ``beside oneself'' with strong emotion.\f*amazement,
\q2 the princes of Judah their rulers, 
\q2 the princes of Zebulun, 
\q2 the princes of Nephthali. 
\q1
\v 29 O God, command your strength: 
\q2 strengthen, O God, this which you have worked in us. 
\q1
\v 30 Because of your temple at Jerusalem 
\q2 kings will bring presents to you. 
\q1
\v 31 Rebuke the beasts of the reed, 
\q2 the crowd of bulls with the heifers of the nations,
\q2 so that those who have been proved with silver may not be shut out: 
\q2 scatter the nations that desire war. 
\q1
\v 32 Ambassadors will arrive out of Egypt; 
\q2 Ethiopia will hasten her hand to God. 
\q1
\v 33 Sing to God, you kingdoms of the earth; 
\q2 sing psalms to the Lord. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 34 Sing to God who rides on the heaven of heaven, eastward: 
\q2 behold, he will give a voice of power.
\q1
\v 35 Give glory to God: 
\q2 his excellency is over Israel, 
\q2 and his power is in the clouds. 
\q1
\v 36 God is wonderful \f + \fr 67:36 \fqa Or, \ft in his holy places.\f*among his holy ones,
\q2 the God of Israel: 
\q2 he will give power and strength to his people: 
\q2 blessed be God. 
\c 68 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning those who will be changed. Of David.
\q1
\v 2 Save me, O God; 
\q2 for the waters have come in to my soul. 
\q1
\v 3 I am stuck fast in deep mud, 
\q2 and there is no standing: 
\q2 I have come in to the depths of the sea, 
\q2 and a storm has overwhelmed me. 
\q1
\v 4 I grew weary crying, 
\q2 my throat has become hoarse; 
\q2 my eyes have failed from waiting for my God. 
\q1
\v 5 Those who hate me without a cause are more than the hairs of my head: 
\q2 my enemies who persecute me unrighteously are strengthened: 
\q2 then I \f + \fr 68:5 \fqa Or, \ft paid for, \fqa or, \ft made up for.\f*restored that which I hadn't take away. 
\q1
\v 6 O God, you know my foolishness; 
\q2 and my transgressions are not hidden from you. 
\q1
\v 7 Don't let those who wait on you, O Lord of hosts, be ashamed on my account: 
\q2 don't let those who seek you be ashamed on my account, O God of Israel. 
\q1
\v 8 For I have suffered reproach for your sake; 
\q2 shame has covered my face. 
\q1
\v 9 I became foreign to my brothers, 
\q2 and a stranger to my mother's children. 
\q1
\v 10 \x + \xo 68:10 \xt John 2. 17; Rom. 15. 3.\x*For the zeal of your house has eaten me up; 
\q2 and the reproaches of those who reproached you have fallen upon me. 
\q1
\v 11 I bowed down my soul with fasting, 
\q2 and that was made my reproach. 
\q1
\v 12 I put on sackcloth for my covering; 
\q2 and I became a proverb to them. 
\q1
\v 13 Those who sit in the gate talked against me, 
\q2 and those who drank wine sang against me. 
\q1
\v 14 ut I—in my prayer to you, O Lord: 
\q2 O God, in a favourable time: 
\q2 in the multitude of your mercy hear me, 
\q2 in the truth of your salvation. 
\q1
\v 15 Save me from the mud, that I not stick:
\q2 let me be delivered from those who hate me, 
\q2 and from the deep waters. 
\q1
\v 16 Don't let the flood of water drown me, 
\q2 nor let the deep swallow me up; 
\q2 neither let the well close its mouth over me. 
\q1
\v 17 Hear me, O Lord; for your mercy is good: 
\q2 according to the multitude of your compassions look upon me. 
\q1
\v 18 Don't turn away your face from your servant; 
\q2 for I am afflicted: 
\q2 hear me speedily. 
\q1
\v 19 Draw near to my soul and redeem it: 
\q2 deliver me because of my enemies. 
\q1
\v 20 For you know my reproach, 
\q2 and my shame, and my confusion; 
\q2 all who afflict me are before you. 
\q1
\v 21 My soul has waited for reproach and misery; 
\q2 and I waited for one to grieve with me, but there was none; 
\q2 and for one to comfort me, but I found none. 
\q1
\v 22 They gave gall for my food, 
\q2 and made me drink vinegar for my thirst. 
\q1
\v 23 \x + \xo 68:23 \xt Rom. 11. 9,10.\x*Let their table before them be for a snare, 
\q2 and for a recompence, 
\q2 and for a stumbling-block. 
\q1
\v 24 Let their eyes be darkened that they should not see; 
\q2 and bow down their back continually. 
\q1
\v 25 Pour out your wrath upon them, 
\q2 and let the fury of your anger take hold on them. 
\q1
\v 26 \x + \xo 68:26 \xt Acts 1. 20.\x*Let their habitation be made desolate; 
\q2 and let there be no inhabitant in their tents: 
\q1
\v 27 because they persecuted him whom you have struck; 
\q2 and they have added to the grief of my wounds. 
\q1
\v 28 Add wickedness to their wickedness; 
\q2 and don't let them come into your righteousness. 
\q1
\v 29 Let them be blotted out of the book of the living, 
\q2 and don't let them be written with the righteous. 
\q1
\v 30 I am poor and sorrowful; 
\q2 but the salvation of your face has helped me. 
\q1
\v 31 I will praise the name of my God with a song, 
\q2 I will magnify him with praise; 
\q1
\v 32 and \add this\add* will please God 
\q2 more than a young calf having horns and hoofs. 
\q1
\v 33 Let the poor see and rejoice; 
\q2 seek the Lord diligently, and you will live. 
\q1
\v 34 For the Lord hears the poor, 
\q2 and does not despise his bound ones. 
\q1
\v 35 Let the heavens and the earth praise him, 
\q2 the sea, and all things moving in them. 
\q1
\v 36 For God will save Zion, 
\q2 and the cities of Judea will be built; 
\q2 and they will dwell there, 
\q2 and inherit it. 
\q1
\v 37 The seed of his servants will possess it, 
\q2 and those who love his name will dwell therein. 
\c 69 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Of David for a remembrance, that the Lord may save me. 
\q1
\v 2 Draw near, O God, to my help. 
\q1
\v 3 Let those be ashamed and greatly agitated who seek my soul: 
\q2 let them be turned backward and put to shame, who wish me evil. 
\q1
\v 4 Let those who say to me, Aha, aha, 
\q2 be turned back and put to shame immediately. 
\q1
\v 5 Let all who seek you exult and be glad in you: 
\q2 and let those that love your salvation say continually, 
\q2 Let God be magnified. 
\q1
\v 6 But I am poor and needy; 
\q2 O God, help me: 
\q2 you are my helper and deliverer, 
\q2 O Lord, don't delay. 
\c 70 
\d
\v 1 Of David. Of the sons of Jonadab, and the first to be taken captive. 
\q1
\v 1b O Lord, I have hoped in you: 
\q2 let me never be put to shame. 
\q1
\v 2 In your righteousness deliver me and rescue me: 
\q2 incline your ear to me, and save me. 
\q1
\v 3 Be to me a protecting God, and a stronghold to save me: 
\q2 for you are my fortress and my refuge. 
\q1
\v 4 Deliver me, O my God, from the hand of the sinner, 
\q2 from the hand of the transgressor and unjust man. 
\q1
\v 5 For you are my support, O Lord; 
\q2 O Lord, my hope from my youth. 
\q1
\v 6 On you have I been stayed from the womb: 
\q2 from the belly of my mother you are my protector: 
\q2 of you is my hymn-singing continually. 
\q1
\v 7 I have become as it were a wonder to many; 
\q2 and you are a strong helper. 
\q1
\v 8 Let my mouth be filled with praise, 
\q2 that I may sing your glory, 
\q2 your majesty all the day. 
\q1
\v 9 Don't cast me off at the time of old age; 
\q2 don't forsake me when my strength fails. 
\q1
\v 10 For my enemies have spoken against me; 
\q2 and those who lay in wait for my soul have taken counsel together, 
\q1
\v 11 saying, God has forsaken him: 
\q2 persecute and take him; 
\q2 for there is no one to deliver. 
\q1
\v 12 O God, don't go far from me, 
\q2 O my God, draw near to my help. 
\q1
\v 13 Let those who plot against my soul be ashamed and completely fail: 
\q2 let those that seek my hurt be clothed with shame and dishonour. 
\q1
\v 14 But I will hope continually, 
\q2 and will praise you more and more. 
\q1
\v 15 My mouth will declare your righteousness openly, 
\q2 your salvation all the day; 
\q2 for I have not known learning.
\q1
\v 16 I will go on in the might of the Lord: 
\q2 O Lord, I will make mention of your righteousness only. 
\q1
\v 17 O God, you have taught me from my youth, 
\q2 and until now will I declare your wonders; 
\q1
\v 18 even until I am old and advanced in years. 
\q2 O God, don't forsake me; 
\q2 until I will have declared your arm to all the generation that is to come: 
\q1
\v 19 even your power and your righteousness, O God, 
\q2 to the heights,
\q2 the mighty works which you have done: 
\q2 O God, who is like you? 
\q1
\v 20 How many afflictions, numerous and severe, you have shown me!
\q2 Yet you turned and revived me, 
\q2 and brought me again from the depths of the earth. 
\q1
\v 21 You multiplied your righteousness, 
\q2 and turned and comforted me, 
\q2 and brought me again out of the depths of the earth. 
\q1
\v 22 For I also will give thanks to you,
\q2 for your truth, O God, 
\q2 on a psaltery:
\q2 I will sing psalms to you on the harp, 
\q2 O Holy One of Israel. 
\q1
\v 23 My lips will rejoice when I sing to you; 
\q2 and my soul, which you have redeemed. 
\q1
\v 24 Moreover also my tongue will rehearse your righteousness all day long;
\q2 when those who seek my hurt are ashamed and counfounded. 
\c 71 
\d
\v 1 For Solomon. 
\q1
\v 1b O God, give your judgment to the king, 
\q2 and your righteousness to the king's son; 
\q1
\v 2 to judge your people with righteousness, 
\q2 and your poor with justice. 
\q1
\v 3 Let the mountains and the hills raise peace to your people: 
\q1
\v 4 he will judge the poor of the people in righteousness, 
\q2 and save the children of the needy; 
\q2 and will bring low the false accuser. 
\q1
\v 5 He will continue as long as the sun, 
\q2 and before the moon forever. 
\q1
\v 6 He will come down as rain upon a fleece; 
\q2 and as drops falling upon the earth. 
\q1
\v 7 In his days righteousness will spring up; 
\q2 and abundance of peace till the moon be removed. 
\q1
\v 8 He will have dominion from sea to sea, 
\q2 and from the River to the ends of the earth. 
\q1
\v 9 The Ethiopians will fall down before him; 
\q2 and his enemies will lick the dust. 
\q1
\v 10 The kings of Tharsis, and the isles, will bring presents: 
\q2 the kings of the Arabs and Saba will offer gifts. 
\q1
\v 11 All kings will worship him; 
\q2 all the nations will serve him. 
\q1
\v 12 For he has delivered the poor from the oppressor; 
\q2 and the needy who had no helper. 
\q1
\v 13 He will spare the poor and needy, 
\q2 and will deliver the souls of the needy. 
\q1
\v 14 He will redeem their souls from usury and injustice: 
\q2 and their name is precious before him. 
\q1
\v 15 He will live, and he will be given gold of Arabia: 
\q2 and they will pray for him continually; 
\q2 all day long they will praise him. 
\q1
\v 16 There will be an establishment on the earth on the tops of the mountains: 
\q2 the fruit of it will be exalted above Lebanon, 
\q2 and from the city they will flourish like grass of the earth. 
\q1
\v 17 Let his name be blessed forever: 
\q2 his name will endure before the sun: 
\q2 and all the tribes of the earth will be blessed in him: 
\q2 all nations will call him blessed.
\b 
\q1
\v 18 Blessed is the Lord God of Israel, 
\q2 who alone does wonders. 
\q1
\v 19 Blessed is his glorious name forever, 
\q2 even forever and ever: 
\q2 and all the earth will be filled with his glory. 
\q0 So be it, so be it. 
\d
\v 20 The hymns of David the son of Jessæ are ended. 
\c 72 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of Asaph.
\q1
\v 1b How good is God to Israel, 
\q2 to the upright in heart! 
\q1
\v 2 But my feet were almost overthrown; 
\q2 my steps were very nearly swept away.
\q1
\v 3 For I envied the transgressors, 
\q2 seeing the peace of sinners. 
\q1
\v 4 For there is no struggle in their death: 
\q2 and there is firmness in their affliction. 
\q1
\v 5 They are not in the troubles of \add other\add* men; 
\q2 and they will not be scourged with \add other\add* men. 
\q1
\v 6 Therefore pride has possessed them; 
\q2 they have clothed themselves with their injustice and ungodliness. 
\q1
\v 7 Their injustice will go forth as out of \f + \fr 72:7 \ft fatness: a common biblical image for prosperity, abundance, and self-indulgent ease.\f*fatness: 
\q2 they have fulfilled their intention. 
\q1
\v 8 They have taken counsel and spoken in wickedness: 
\q2 they have uttered unrighteousness loftily. 
\q1
\v 9 They have set their mouth against heaven, 
\q2 and their tongue has gone throughout the earth. 
\q1
\v 10 Therefore my people will return here: 
\q2 and full days will be found for them. 
\q1
\v 11 They said, How does God know? 
\q2 And is there knowledge in the Most High? 
\q1
\v 12 Behold, these sinners also prosper; 
\q2 they have possessed wealth forever. 
\q1
\v 13 I said, Truly in vain I have justified my heart, 
\q2 and washed my hands in innocence. 
\q1
\v 14 For I was plagued all day long, 
\q2 and my correction \add came\add* every morning. 
\q1
\v 15 If I said, I will speak thus; 
\q2 behold, I would have broken faith with the generation of your children. 
\q1
\v 16 I undertook to understand this, 
\q2 it was a burden to me, 
\q1
\v 17 until I entered the sanctuary of God; 
\q2 and understood their end. 
\q1
\v 18 Surely because of their schemes you have set \add this\add* for them: 
\q2 you have cast them down when they were lifted up. 
\q1
\v 19 How they have become desolate! 
\q2 Suddenly they have failed: 
\q2 they have perished because of their wickedness. 
\q1
\v 20 Like a dream when one awakes, O Lord, 
\q2 in your city you will bring their image to nothing. 
\q1
\v 21 For my heart burned, 
\q2 and my kidneys were changed.
\q1
\v 22 But I, having been brought to nothing, didn't know; 
\q2 I became brutish before you. 
\q1
\v 23 Yet I am continually with you: 
\q2 you have held my right hand. 
\q1
\v 24 You have guided me by your counsel, 
\q2 and you have taken me to yourself with glory. 
\q1
\v 25 For what do I have in heaven, 
\q2 and beside you, what have I desired upon the earth? 
\q1
\v 26 My heart and my flesh have failed: 
\q2 God is God of my heart, 
\q2 and my portion forever. 
\q1
\v 27 For, behold, those who remove themselves far from you will perish: 
\q2 you have destroyed every one who plays the harlot away from you. 
\q1
\v 28 But it is good for me to cling to God, 
\q2 to put my hope in the Lord; 
\q2 that I may proclaim all your praise in the gates of the daughter of Zion. 
\c 73 
\d
\v 1 For understanding. Of Asaph.
\q1
\v 1b Why have you rejected, O God, forever? 
\q2 Your wrath is kindled against the sheep of your pasture.
\q1
\v 2 Remember your congregation which you have purchased from the beginning; 
\q2 you ransomed the rod of your inheritance; 
\q2 this mount Zion where you have dwelt. 
\q1
\v 3 Lift up your hands to put an end to their arrogant deeds; 
\q2 all the evil that the enemy has done in your holy places. 
\q1
\v 4 Those who hate you have boasted in the midst of your feast; 
\q2 they set up their signs as signs,
\q1
\v 5 and they did not know
\q2 \f + \fr 73:5--6 \ft The English is obscure here because the Greek is also obscure.\f*as in the entrance above; 
\q1
\v 6 as in a forest of trees
\q2 they cut down its doors with axes; 
\q2 together they have shattered it with hatchet and stone cutter. 
\q1
\v 7 They have burnt your sanctuary with fire to the ground; 
\q2 they have profaned the tabernacle of your name. 
\q1
\v 8 They have said in their heart, 
\q2 all their kindred together, 
\q2 Come, let us destroy the feasts of the Lord from the earth. 
\q1
\v 9 We have not seen our signs; 
\q2 there is no longer a prophet; 
\q2 and he will not know us any more. 
\q1
\v 10 How long, O God, will the enemy reproach? 
\q2 Will the enemy provoke your name forever? 
\q1
\v 11 Why do you turn away your hand, 
\q2 and keep your right hand in your bosom forever? 
\q1
\v 12 But God is our King \f + \fr 73:12 \fqa Gr. \ft before the age.\f*of old; 
\q2 he has worked salvation in the midst of the earth. 
\q1
\v 13 You established the sea, in your might, 
\q2 you broke to pieces the heads of the \f + \fr 73:13 \fqa Gr. \ft δρακόντων, traditionally 'dragons'; here, mighty sea creatures.\f*mighty serpents in the water. 
\q1
\v 14 You broke to pieces the heads of the serpent; 
\q2 you gave him as food to the Ethiopian nations. 
\q1
\v 15 You split open fountains and torrents; 
\q2 you dried up the rivers of Etham.
\q1
\v 16 The day is yours, and the night is yours; 
\q2 you have prepared the \f + \fr 73:16 \fqa luminary: \ft The Greek has a general word for a source of light, traditionally understood here as the moon.\f*luminary and the sun.
\q1
\v 17 You have made all the borders of the earth; 
\q2 you have made summer and spring. 
\q1
\v 18 Remember this: 
\q2 an enemy has reproached the Lord, 
\q2 and a foolish people has provoked your name. 
\q1
\v 19 Don't deliver to the wild beasts a soul that gives praise to you: 
\q2 don't forget forever the souls of your poor. 
\q1
\v 20 Look upon your covenant: 
\q2 for the dark \add places\add* of the earth are filled with dwellings of wickedness. 
\q1
\v 21 Don't let the afflicted and shamed one be rejected: 
\q2 the poor and needy will praise your name. 
\q1
\v 22 Arise, O God, plead your cause: 
\q2 remember your reproaches that come from the foolish one all day long. 
\q1
\v 23 Don't forget the voice of your petitioners: 
\q2 the pride of those who hate you continually rises before you. 
\c 74 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Don't destroy. A Psalm of Asaph, of a song.
\q1
\v 2 We will give thanks to you, O God, 
\q2 we will give thanks, and call upon your name: 
\q2 I will declare all your wonderful works. 
\q1
\v 3 When I decide it's time, 
\q2 I will judge righteously. 
\q1
\v 4 The earth is dissolved, and all who dwell in it: 
\q2 I have strengthened its pillars. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 I said to the transgressors, Do not transgress; 
\q2 and to the sinners, Don't lift up the horn. 
\q1
\v 6 Don't lift up your horn on high; 
\q2 don't speak unrighteousness against God. 
\q1
\v 7 For \add it comes\add* neither from the east, 
\q2 nor from the west, 
\q2 nor from the \f + \fr 74:7 \fqa Gr. \ft deserted or wilderness mountain regions.\f*remote mountains, 
\q1
\v 8 for God is the judge; 
\q2 he puts down one, and raises up another. 
\q1
\v 9 For \add there is\add* in the hand of the Lord a cup of undiluted wine, full of rich mixture;
\q2 and he has poured from this into that, 
\q2 but its dregs have not been emptied out; 
\q2 all the sinners of the earth will drink. 
\q1
\v 10 But I will exult forever: 
\q2 I will sing praises to the God of Jacob. 
\q1
\v 11 I will break all the horns of sinners; 
\q2 but the horns of the righteous one will be exalted. 
\c 75 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Among the Hymns. A Psalm for Asaph; a Song for the Assyrian. 
\q1
\v 2 God is known in Judea;
\q2 his name is great in Israel. 
\q1
\v 3 His place has been in peace, 
\q2 and his dwelling-place in Zion. 
\q1
\v 4 There he broke the power of the bows, 
\q2 the shield, and the sword, and the battle. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 You shine forth wonderfully from the everlasting mountains. 
\q1
\v 6 All the foolish in heart were troubled; 
\q2 they slept their sleep, 
\q2 and all the wealthy men found nothing in their hands. 
\q1
\v 7 At your rebuke, O God of Jacob, 
\q2 the riders on horses slumbered. 
\q1
\v 8 You are to be feared; 
\q2 and who will withstand you, because of your anger? 
\q1
\v 9 You caused judgment to be heard from heaven; 
\q2 the earth feared, and was still, 
\q1
\v 10 when God arose to judgment, 
\q2 to save all the meek in heart. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 11 For the inward thought of man will give thanks to you: 
\q2 and the memorial of his inward thought will keep a feast to you. 
\q1
\v 12 Vow, and pay \add your vows\add* to the Lord our God; 
\q2 all who are all around him will bring gifts, to him that is to be feared, 
\q1
\v 13 and that takes away the spirits of princes;
\q2 to him that is to be feared among the kings of the earth. 
\c 76 
\d
\v 1 For the end. For Idithun. A Psalm of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 2 I cried to the Lord with my voice,
\q2 yes, my voice was to God; 
\q2 and he gave heed to me. 
\q1
\v 3 In the day of my affliction I earnestly sought God, 
\q2 with my hands before him by night, 
\q2 and I was not deceived; 
\q2 my soul refused to be comforted. 
\q1
\v 4 I remembered God, and rejoiced; 
\q2 I poured out my complaint, 
\q2 and my soul fainted. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 5 My eyes anticipated the night watches; 
\q2 I was troubled, and didn't speak. 
\q1
\v 6 I considered the days of old, 
\q2 and remembered ancient years. 
\q1
\v 7 I meditated; 
\q2 I communed with my heart by night, 
\q2 and diligently searched my spirit,  
\q1
\v 8 Will the Lord cast off forever? 
\q2 And will he be well pleased no more? 
\q1
\v 9 Will he cut off his mercy forever, 
\q2 even forever and ever? 
\q1
\v 10 Will God forget to pity? 
\q2 Or will he shut up his compassions in his wrath? \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 11 I said, Now I have begun; 
\q2 this is the change of the right hand of the Most High. 
\q1
\v 12 I remembered the works of the Lord; 
\q2 for I will remember your wonders from the beginning. 
\q1
\v 13 I will meditate on all your works, 
\q2 and will consider your doings. 
\q1
\v 14 O God, your way is in the sanctuary; 
\q2 who is a great God like our God? 
\q1
\v 15 You are the God who does wonders; 
\q2 you have made known your power among the nations. 
\q1
\v 16 With your arm you have redeemed your people, 
\q2 the sons of Jacob and Joseph. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 17 The waters saw you, O God, 
\q2 the waters saw you, and feared; 
\q2 and the depths were troubled. 
\q1
\v 18 An abundant sound of waters: 
\q2 the clouds uttered a voice; 
\q2 for your arrows went abroad. 
\q1
\v 19 The voice of your thunder was in the \f + \fr 76:19 \fqa Gr. \ft wheel.\f*whirlwind, 
\q2 and your lightnings appeared to the world; 
\q2 the earth trembled and quaked. 
\q1
\v 20 Your way is in the sea, 
\q2 and your paths in many waters, 
\q2 and your footsteps cannot be known. 
\q1
\v 21 You guided your people as sheep 
\q2 by the hand of Moses and Aaron. 
\c 77 
\d
\v 1 Of understanding. Of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 1b Give heed, O my people, to my law: 
\q2 incline your ear to the words of my mouth. 
\q1
\v 2 \x + \xo 77:2 \xt Mat. 13. 35.\x*I will open my mouth in parables: 
\q2 I will utter riddles from of old. 
\q1
\v 3 All which we have heard and known, 
\q2 and our fathers have declared to us. 
\q1
\v 4 They were not hidden from their children,
\q2 declaring to another generation the praises of the Lord, and his mighty acts, 
\q2 and his wonders which he worked. 
\q1
\v 5 He raised up a testimony in Jacob, 
\q2 and appointed a law in Israel, 
\q2 which he commanded our fathers, 
\q2 to make it known to their children: 
\q1
\v 6 that another generation might know, 
\q2 even the sons which should be born; 
\q2 and they should arise and declare them to their children. 
\q1
\v 7 That they might set their hope on God, 
\q2 and not forget the works of God, 
\q2 but diligently seek his commandments. 
\q1
\v 8 That they should not be like their fathers, 
\q2 a perverse and provoking generation; 
\q2 a generation which didn't set its heart aright, 
\q2 and its spirit was not faithful with God. 
\q1
\v 9 The children of Ephraim, 
\q2 bending and shooting the bow, 
\q2 turned back in the day of battle. 
\q1
\v 10 They didn't keep the covenant of God, 
\q2 and would not walk in his law. 
\q1
\v 11 They forgot his benefits, 
\q2 and his miracles which he had shown them; 
\q1
\v 12 the miracles which he worked before their fathers, 
\q2 in the land of Egypt, in the plain of Tanes. 
\q1
\v 13 He split the sea, and led them through: 
\q2 he made the waters to stand like a wineskin. 
\q1
\v 14 He guided them with a cloud by day, 
\q2 and all night with light of a fire. 
\q1
\v 15 He split a rock in the wilderness, 
\q2 and made them drink as from a great deep. 
\q1
\v 16 He brought water out of the rock, 
\q2 and caused waters to flow down as rivers. 
\q1
\v 17 They sinned still more against him; 
\q2 they provoked the Most High in the wilderness. 
\q1
\v 18 They tempted God in their hearts, 
\q2 by asking food for their souls. 
\q1
\v 19 They also spoke against God, 
\q2 and said, Will God be able to prepare a table in the wilderness? 
\q1
\v 20 Although he struck the rock, 
\q2 and the waters flowed, 
\q2 and the torrents ran abundantly; 
\q2 will he be able to also give bread, 
\q2 or prepare a table for his people? 
\q1
\v 21 Therefore the Lord heard, and was provoked: 
\q2 and fire was kindled in Jacob, 
\q2 and wrath went up against Israel. 
\q1
\v 22 Because they didn't believe in God, 
\q2 and didn't trust in his salvation. 
\q1
\v 23 Yet he commanded the clouds from above, 
\q2 and opened the doors of heaven, 
\q1
\v 24 and rained upon them manna to eat, 
\q2 and gave them the bread of heaven. 
\q1
\v 25 Man ate angels' bread; 
\q2 \x + \xo 77:25 \xt Mat. 6. 31.\x*he sent them provision to the full. 
\q1
\v 26 He removed the south wind from heaven; 
\q2 and by his might he brought in the south-west wind. 
\q1
\v 27 He rained upon them flesh like dust, 
\q2 and feathered birds like the sand of the seas. 
\q1
\v 28 They fell into the midst of their camp, 
\q2 all around their tents. 
\q1
\v 29 So they ate, and were completely filled; 
\q2 and he gave them their desire. 
\q1
\v 30 They were not denied their desire. 
\q2 While their food was still in their mouth, 
\q1
\v 31 then the wrath of God rose against them, 
\q2 and killed the fattest of them, 
\q2 and brought low the chosen men of Israel. 
\q1
\v 32 Among all this they sinned still more, 
\q2 and didn't believe his miracles. 
\q1
\v 33 Their days were consumed in vanity, 
\q2 and their years with anxiety. 
\q1
\v 34 When he killed them, they sought him: 
\q2 and they returned and sought God early.
\q1
\v 35 They remembered that God was their helper, 
\q2 and the most high God was their redeemer. 
\q1
\v 36 Yet they loved him with their mouth, 
\q2 and lied to him with their tongue. 
\q1
\v 37 For their heart was not right with him, 
\q2 neither were they steadfast in his covenant. 
\q1
\v 38 But he is compassionate, and atones for their sins, 
\q2 and does not destroy: 
\q2 yes, he frequently turns away his wrath, 
\q2 and does not kindle all his anger. 
\q1
\v 39 He remembered that they are flesh; 
\q2 a wind that passes away, and doesn't return. 
\q1
\v 40 How often did they provoke him in the wilderness, 
\q2 and anger him in a dry land! 
\q1
\v 41 Yes, they turned back, and tempted God, 
\q2 and provoked the Holy One of Israel. 
\q1
\v 42 They didn't remember his hand, 
\q2 the day in which he delivered them from the hand of the oppressor. 
\q1
\v 43 How he had worked his signs in Egypt, 
\q2 and his wonders in the field of Tanes: 
\q1
\v 44 and had changed their rivers into blood; 
\q2 and their rainwaters, that they should not drink. 
\q1
\v 45 He sent against them the dog-fly, and it devoured them; 
\q2 and the frog, and it spoiled them. 
\q1
\v 46 He gave their fruit to the canker worm, 
\q2 and their labours to the locust. 
\q1
\v 47 He killed their vines with hail, 
\q2 and their sycamores with frost. 
\q1
\v 48 He gave up their livestock to hail, 
\q2 and their possessions to the fire. 
\q1
\v 49 He sent out against them the fury of his anger, 
\q2 wrath, and indignation, and affliction, a message by evil angels. 
\q1
\v 50 He made a way for his wrath; 
\q2 he didn't spare their souls from death, but consigned their cattle to death; 
\q1
\v 51 and struck every first-born in the land of Egypt; 
\q2 the first-fruits of their labours in the tents of Ham. 
\q1
\v 52 He removed his people like sheep; 
\q2 he led them as a flock in the wilderness. 
\q1
\v 53 He guided them with hope, and they didn't fear: 
\q2 but the sea covered their enemies. 
\q1
\v 54 He brought them in to the mountain of his sanctuary, 
\q2 this mountain which his right hand had purchased. 
\q1
\v 55 He cast out the nations from before them, 
\q2 and gave them an inheritance by a measuring line, 
\q2 and made the tribes of Israel to dwell in their tents. 
\q1
\v 56 Yet they tempted and provoked the most high God, 
\q2 and didn't keep his testimonies. 
\q1
\v 57 They turned back, and broke covenant, just as their fathers also had:
\q2 they became like a crooked bow. 
\q1
\v 58 They provoked him with their high places, 
\q2 and moved him to jealousy with their graven images. 
\q1
\v 59 God heard and ignored them,
\q2 and greatly despised Israel. 
\q1
\v 60 He rejected the tabernacle of Shiloh, 
\q2 his tent where he dwelt among men. 
\q1
\v 61 He gave their strength into captivity, 
\q2 and their beauty into the enemy's hand. 
\q1
\v 62 He \f + \fr 77:62 \fqa Gr. \ft shut up.\f*gave his people to the sword; 
\q2 and ignored his inheritance. 
\q1
\v 63 Fire devoured their young men; 
\q2 and their virgins were not mourned. 
\q1
\v 64 Their priests fell by the sword; 
\q2 and their widows will not be wept for. 
\q1
\v 65 So the Lord awoke as one out of sleep, 
\q2 like a mighty man who has been heated with wine. 
\q1
\v 66 He struck his enemies from behind: 
\q2 he gave them a perpetual reproach. 
\q1
\v 67 He rejected the tabernacle of Joseph, 
\q2 and didn't choose the tribe of Ephraim; 
\q1
\v 68 but chose the tribe of Judah, 
\q2 the mount Zion which he loved. 
\q1
\v 69 He built his sanctuary as \f + \fr 77:69 \fqa Gr. \ft μονοκερώτων “wild oxen”; traditionally “unicorns”. The comparison is obscure.\f*of wild oxen; 
\q2 he founded it forever on the earth. 
\q1
\v 70 He chose David his servant, 
\q2 and took him up from the flocks of sheep. 
\q1
\v 71 He took him from following the ewes great with young, 
\q2 to be the shepherd of Jacob his servant, and Israel his inheritance. 
\q1
\v 72 So he tended them in the integrity of his heart; 
\q2 and guided them by the skillfulness of his hands. 
\c 78 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm Of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 1b O God, the nations have come into your inheritance; 
\q2 they have polluted your holy temple; 
\q2 they have made Jerusalem a watchman's hut. 
\q1
\v 2 They have given the dead bodies of your servants as food for the birds of the sky, 
\q2 the flesh of your holy ones for the wild beasts of the earth. 
\q1
\v 3 They have shed their blood as water, all around Jerusalem; 
\q2 and there was no one to bury. 
\q1
\v 4 We have become a reproach to our neighbours, 
\q2 a scorn and derision to those all around us. 
\q1
\v 5 How long, O Lord? Will you be angry forever? 
\q2 Will your zeal burn like fire? 
\q1
\v 6 Pour out your wrath upon the nations that have not known you, 
\q2 and upon the kingdoms which have not called upon your name. 
\q1
\v 7 For they have devoured Jacob, 
\q2 and laid his place waste. 
\q1
\v 8 Don't remember our old transgressions; 
\q2 let your tender mercies speedily meet us; 
\q2 for we are greatly impoverished. 
\q1
\v 9 Help us, O God our Saviour; 
\q2 for the glory of your name, O Lord, deliver us; 
\q2 and be merciful to our sins, for your name's sake. 
\q1
\v 10 Lest the nations say, Where is their God? 
\q2 And let the avenging of the shed blood of your servants be known among the nations before our eyes. 
\q1
\v 11 Let the groaning of the prisoners come in before you; 
\q2 according to the greatness of your arm preserve the sons of the killed ones. 
\q1
\v 12 Repay to our neighbours sevenfold into their bosom their reproach, 
\q2 with which they have reproached you, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 13 For we are your people and the sheep of your pasture; 
\q2 we will give you thanks forever; 
\q2 we will declare your praise throughout all generations. 
\c 79 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning the things that will be changed. A testimony of Asaph. A Psalm concerning the Assyrian. 
\q1
\v 2 Pay attention, O Shepherd of Israel, who guides Joseph like a flock; 
\q2 you who sit upon the cherubs, manifest yourself; 
\q1
\v 3 before Ephraim and Benjamin and Manasseh, 
\q2 stir up your power, and come to deliver us. 
\q1
\v 4 O God, turn us,
\q2 and cause your face to shine; 
\q2 and we will be saved. 
\q1
\v 5 O Lord God of hosts, how long are you angry 
\q2 with the prayer of your servant? 
\q1
\v 6 You will feed us with bread of tears; 
\q2 and will cause us to drink tears by measure. 
\q1
\v 7 You have made us a strife to our neighbours; 
\q2 and our enemies have mocked at us. 
\q1
\v 8 O Lord God of hosts, turn us,
\q2 and cause your face to shine; 
\q2 and we will be saved. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 You have transplanted a vine out of Egypt: 
\q2 you have cast out the nations, and planted it. 
\q1
\v 10 You made a way before it, and caused its roots to strike, 
\q2 and the land was filled.
\q1
\v 11 Its shadow covered the mountains, 
\q2 and its shoots the cedars of God. 
\q1
\v 12 It sent its branches to the sea, 
\q2 and its shoots to the River. 
\q1
\v 13 Why have you broken down its hedge, 
\q2 while all who pass by the way pluck it? 
\q1
\v 14 The boar out of the wood has laid it waste, 
\q2 and a wild beast has fed upon it. 
\q1
\v 15 O God of hosts, turn, we pray you: 
\q2 look down from heaven, and see, and visit this vine; 
\q1
\v 16 and restore that which your right hand has planted: 
\q2 and look on the son of man whom you strengthened for yourself. 
\q1
\v 17 It has been burnt with fire and dug up: 
\q2 they will perish at the rebuke of your presence. 
\q1
\v 18 Let your hand be upon the man on your right, 
\q2 and upon the son of man whom you strengthened for yourself. 
\q1
\v 19 So we will surely not depart from you: 
\q2 you will revive us, and we will call upon your name. 
\q1
\v 20 O Lord God of hosts, turn us,
\q2 and cause your face to shine; 
\q2 and we will be saved. 
\c 80 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning the wine-presses. A Psalm of \f + \fr 80:1 \fqa Alex. \ft David.\f*Asaph.
\q1
\v 2 Rejoice in God our helper; 
\q2 shout aloud to the God of Jacob. 
\q1
\v 3 Take a psalm, and produce the timbrel, 
\q2 the pleasant psaltery with the harp. 
\q1
\v 4 Blow the trumpet at the new moon, 
\q2 in the glorious day of \f + \fr 80:4 \fqa Alex. \ft our.\f*your feast. 
\q1
\v 5 For it is an ordinance for Israel, 
\q2 and a statute of the God of Jacob. 
\q1
\v 6 He established him as a testimony in Joseph,
\q2 when he came forth out of the land of Egypt: 
\q2 he heard a language he didn't understand. 
\q1
\v 7 He removed his back from burdens: 
\q2 his hands laboured at the basket. 
\q1
\v 8 You called upon me in trouble, and I delivered you; 
\q2 I answered you from the secret place of the storm: 
\q2 I proved you at the water of Strife. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 Hear, my people, and I will speak to you, O Israel; 
\q2 and I will testify to you: if you will listen to me; 
\q1
\v 10 there will be no new god in you; 
\q2 neither will you worship a foreign god. 
\q1
\v 11 For I am the Lord your God who brought you out of the land of Egypt: 
\q2 open your mouth wide, and I will fill it. 
\q1
\v 12 But my people didn't listen to my voice; 
\q2 and Israel gave no heed to me. 
\q1
\v 13 So I let them go after the ways of their own hearts: 
\q2 they will go on in their own ways. 
\q1
\v 14 If my people had listened to me, 
\q2 if Israel had walked in my ways, 
\q1
\v 15 I would have put down their enemies very quickly, 
\q2 and would have laid my hand upon those who afflicted them. 
\q1
\v 16 The Lord's enemies were false to him; 
\q2 and their time will be forever. 
\q1
\v 17 He fed them with the fat of wheat; 
\q2 and satisfied them with honey out of the rock. 
\c 81 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 1b God stands in the assembly of gods; 
\q2 and in the midst he judges gods. 
\q1
\v 2 How long will you judge unrighteously, 
\q2 and show partiality to sinners? \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 3 Judge the orphan and poor: 
\q2 do justice to the low and needy. 
\q1
\v 4 Rescue the needy, 
\q2 and deliver the poor out of the hand of the sinner. 
\q1
\v 5 They don't know, nor understand; 
\q2 they walk on in darkness: 
\q2 all the foundations of the earth will be shaken. 
\q1
\v 6 I have said, You are gods, 
\q2 and all children of the Most High. 
\q1
\v 7 But you will die as men, 
\q2 and fall as one of the rulers. 
\q1
\v 8 Arise, O God, judge the earth: 
\q2 for you will inherit among all the nations. 
\c 82 
\d
\v 1 A Song of a Psalm of Asaph. 
\q1
\v 2 O God, who will be compared to you?
\q2 Don't be silent, neither be still, O God. 
\q1
\v 3 For behold, your enemies have made a noise; 
\q2 and those who hate you have lifted up the head. 
\q1
\v 4 Against your people they have craftily imagined a scheme, 
\q2 and have taken counsel against your saints. 
\q1
\v 5 They have said, Come, and let us completely destroy them out of the nation; 
\q2 and let the name of Israel be remembered no more at all. 
\q1
\v 6 For they have taken counsel together with one consent: 
\q2 they have made a confederacy against you; 
\q1
\v 7 even the tents of the Idumeans, and the Ismaelites; 
\q2 Moab, and the Agarenes; 
\q1
\v 8 Gebal, and Ammon, and Amalec; 
\q2 the Philistines also, with those who dwell at Tyre. 
\q1
\v 9 Yes, Assur too has come with them: 
\q2 they have become a help to the children of Lot. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 10 Do you to them as to Midian, and to Sisera; 
\q2 as to Jabin at the brook of Kison. 
\q1
\v 11 They were completely destroyed at Aendor: 
\q2 they became as dung for the earth. 
\q1
\v 12 Make their princes as Oreb and Zeb, 
\q2 and Zebee and Salmana; all their princes: 
\q1
\v 13 who said, Let us take to ourselves the \f + \fr 82:13 \fqa Alex. \ft sanctuary.\f*altar of God as an inheritance. 
\q1
\v 14 O my God, make them as a wheel; 
\q2 as stubble before the wind. 
\q1
\v 15 As fire which will burn up a wood, 
\q2 as the flame may consume the mountains; 
\q1
\v 16 so will you persecute them with your tempest, 
\q2 and trouble them in your anger. 
\q1
\v 17 Fill their faces with dishonour; 
\q2 so they will seek your name, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 18 Let them be ashamed and troubled for evermore; 
\q2 yes, let them be greatly agitated and destroyed. 
\q1
\v 19 Let them know that your name is Lord; 
\q2 that you alone are Most High over all the earth. 
\c 83 
\d
\v 1 For the end. Concerning the wine-presses. A Psalm of the sons of Korah.
\q1
\v 2 How lovely are your dwelling places, 
\q2 O Lord of hosts! 
\q1
\v 3 My soul longs, and faints for the courts of the Lord: 
\q2 my heart and my flesh have exulted in the living God. 
\q1
\v 4 Yes, the sparrow has found himself a home, 
\q2 and the turtle-dove a nest for herself, where she may lay her young: 
\q2 your altars, O Lord of hosts, my King, and my God. 
\q1
\v 5 Blessed are those who dwell in your house: 
\q2 they will praise you evermore. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 Blessed is the man whose help is of you, O Lord; 
\q2 he has purposed ascents in his heart
\q1
\v 7 in the valley of weeping, to the place which he has appointed, 
\q2 for the law-giver will give blessings. 
\q1
\v 8 They will go from strength to strength: 
\q2 the God of gods will be seen in Zion. 
\q1
\v 9 O Lord God of hosts, hear my prayer: 
\q2 listen, O God of Jacob. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 10 Behold, O God our defender, 
\q2 and look upon the face of your anointed. 
\q1
\v 11 For one day in your courts is better than thousands. 
\q2 I chose to be on the margins in the house of God, rather than dwell in the tents of sinners. 
\q1
\v 12 For the Lord loves mercy and truth: 
\q2 God will give grace and glory: 
\q2 the Lord will not withhold good things from those who walk in innocence. 
\q1
\v 13 O Lord of hosts, 
\q2 blessed is the man who trusts in you. 
\c 84 
\d
\v 1 For the end. A Psalm of the sons of Korah. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord, you have taken pleasure in your land: 
\q2 you have turned back the captivity of Jacob. 
\q1
\v 3 You have forgiven your people their transgressions; 
\q2 you have covered all their sins. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 You have caused all your wrath to cease: 
\q2 you have turned from your fierce anger. 
\q1
\v 5 Turn us, O God of our salvation, 
\q2 and turn your anger away from us. 
\q1
\v 6 Would you be angry with us forever? 
\q2 Or will you continue your wrath from generation to generation? 
\q1
\v 7 O God, you will turn and revive us; 
\q2 and your people will rejoice in you. 
\q1
\v 8 Show us your mercy, O Lord, 
\q2 and grant us your salvation. 
\q1
\v 9 I will hear what the Lord God will say concerning me: 
\q2 for he will speak peace to his people, and to his saints, 
\q2 and to those that turn their heart toward him. 
\q1
\v 10 Moreover his salvation is near those who fear him; 
\q2 that glory may dwell in our land. 
\q1
\v 11 Mercy and truth are met together: 
\q2 righteousness and peace have kissed. 
\q1
\v 12 Truth has sprung out of the earth; 
\q2 and righteousness has looked down from heaven. 
\q1
\v 13 For the Lord will give goodness; 
\q2 and our land will yield her fruit. 
\q1
\v 14 Righteousness will go before him; 
\q2 and will set his steps in the way. 
\c 85 
\d
\v 1 A Prayer of David. 
\q1
\v 1b O Lord, incline your ear, and listen to me; 
\q2 for I am poor and needy. 
\q1
\v 2 Preserve my soul, for I am holy; 
\q2 save your servant, O God, who hopes in you. 
\q1
\v 3 Pity me, O Lord: 
\q2 for to you will I cry all the day. 
\q1
\v 4 Gladden the soul of your servant: 
\q2 for to you, O Lord, I have lifted up my soul. 
\q1
\v 5 For you, O Lord, are kind and gentle; 
\q2 and abounding in mercy to all who call upon you. 
\q1
\v 6 Give ear to my prayer, O Lord; 
\q2 and pay attention to the voice of my supplication. 
\q1
\v 7 In the day of my trouble I cried to you: 
\q2 for you heard me. 
\q1
\v 8 There is no one like you, O Lord, among the gods; 
\q2 and nothing matches your works. 
\q1
\v 9 All nations whom you have made will come, and will worship before you, O Lord; 
\q2 and will glorify your name. 
\q1
\v 10 For you are great, and do wonders: 
\q2 you are the only, the great God. 
\q1
\v 11 Guide me, O Lord, in your way, and I will walk in your truth: 
\q2 let my heart rejoice, that I may fear your name. 
\q1
\v 12 I will give you thanks, O Lord my God, with all my heart; 
\q2 and I will glorify your name forever. 
\q1
\v 13 For your mercy is great toward me; 
\q2 and you have delivered my soul from deepest Hades.
\q1
\v 14 O God, transgressors have risen up against me, 
\q2 and an assembly of violent \add men\add* have sought my life; 
\q2 and have not set you before them. 
\q1
\v 15 But you, O Lord God, are compassionate and merciful, 
\q2 long-suffering, and abundant in mercy, and true. 
\q1
\v 16 Look you upon me, and have mercy upon me: 
\q2 give your strength to your servant, and save the son of your handmaid. 
\q1
\v 17 Establish with me a token for good; 
\q2 and let those who hate me see and be put to shame; 
\q2 because you, O Lord, have helped me, and comforted me. 
\c 86 
\d
\v 1 Of the sons of Korah. A Psalm of a Song. 
\q1
\v 1b His foundations are in the holy mountains. 
\q1
\v 2 The Lord loves the gates of Zion,
\q2 more than all the tents of Jacob. 
\q1
\v 3 Glorious things are spoken of you, 
\q2 O city of God. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 4 I will make mention of Rahab and Babylon to those who know me: 
\q2 behold also the Philistines, and Tyre, and the people of the Ethiopians: 
\q2 these were born there. 
\q1
\v 5 A man will say, Mother Zion;
\q2 and a man was born in her; 
\q2 and the Most High himself founded her. 
\q1
\v 6 The Lord will recount in the register of the peoples and rulers 
\q2 these who were born in her. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 7 All who rejoice have their dwelling in you.
\c 87 
\d
\v 1 A song of a Psalm. Of the sons of Korah. For the end. Upon Maeleth; for responding. Of instruction for Heman the Israelite. 
\q1
\v 2 O Lord God of my salvation, 
\q2 I have cried by day and in the night before you. 
\q1
\v 3 Let my prayer come in before you; 
\q2 incline your ear to my supplication, O Lord. 
\q1
\v 4 For my soul is filled with troubles, 
\q2 and my life has drawn near to Hades. 
\q1
\v 5 I have been reckoned with those who go down to the pit; 
\q2 I became as a man without help, free among the dead; 
\q1
\v 6 as a wounded men cast out, who sleep in the tomb; 
\q2 whom you remember no more; 
\q2 and they are rejected from your hand. 
\q1
\v 7 They laid me in the lowest pit, 
\q2 in the dark, and in the shadow of death. 
\q1
\v 8 Your wrath has pressed heavily upon me, 
\q2 and you have brought all your surging waves upon me. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 9 You have removed my acquaintance far from me; 
\q2 they have made me an abomination to themselves; 
\q2 I have been delivered up, and have not gone forth. 
\q1
\v 10 My eyes are dimmed from poverty; 
\q2 but I cried to you, O Lord, all day long; 
\q2 I spread forth my hands to you. 
\q1
\v 11 Will you work wonders for the dead? 
\q2 Or will physicians raise them up so that they may praise you? 
\q1
\v 12 Will any one declare your mercy in the tomb? 
\q2 And your truth in destruction? 
\q1
\v 13 Will your wonders be known in darkness? 
\q2 And your righteousness in a forgotten land? 
\q1
\v 14 But I cried to you, O Lord; 
\q2 and in the morning my prayer will come before you. 
\q1
\v 15 Why, O Lord, do you reject my soul, 
\q2 and turn your face away from me? 
\q1
\v 16 I am poor and in troubles from my youth; 
\q2 and having been exalted, I was brought low and driven to \f + \fr 87:16 \ft See \xt 2 Cor. 4. 8.\f*despair. 
\q1
\v 17 Your wrath has passed over me; 
\q2 and your terrors have greatly disquieted me. 
\q1
\v 18 They surrounded me like water; 
\q2 all day long they enclosed me altogether. 
\q1
\v 19 You have put far from me friend and neighbour, 
\q2 and my acquaintances because of my affliction. 
\c 88 
\d
\v 1 Of instruction. Of Ethan the Israelite. 
\q1
\v 2 I will sing of your mercies, O Lord, forever: 
\q2 I will declare your truth with my mouth to all generations. 
\q1
\v 3 For you have said, Mercy will be built up forever: 
\q2 your truth will be established in the heavens. 
\q1
\v 4 I made a covenant with my chosen ones, 
\q2 I swore to David my servant. 
\q1
\v 5 I will establish your seed forever, 
\q2 and build up your throne to all generations. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 6 The heavens will declare your wonders, O Lord; 
\q2 and your truth in the assembly of the holy ones. 
\q1
\v 7 For who in the clouds will be compared to the Lord? 
\q2 And who will be likened to the Lord among the sons of God? 
\q1
\v 8 God is glorified in the council of the holy ones; 
\q2 great and fearsome toward all who surround him. 
\q1
\v 9 O Lord God of hosts, who is like to you? 
\q2 You are mighty, O Lord, and your truth is all around you. 
\q1
\v 10 You rule the power of the sea; 
\q2 and you calm the tumult of its waves. 
\q1
\v 11 You have brought down the proud as one that is killed; 
\q2 and with the arm of your power you have scattered your enemies. 
\q1
\v 12 The heavens are yours, and the earth is yours: 
\q2 you founded the world, and its fulness. 
\q1
\v 13 You have created the north and the \f + \fr 88:13 \ft In Hebrew poetry ‘sea’ is sometimes used symbolically of the west.\f*seas: 
\q2 Tabor and Hermon will rejoice in your name. 
\q1
\v 14 Yours is the mighty arm: let your hand be strengthened, 
\q2 let your right hand be exalted. 
\q1
\v 15 Righteousness and justice are the foundation of your throne: 
\q2 mercy and truth go before your face. 
\q1
\v 16 Blessed are the people who know the joyful shout: 
\q2 O Lord, they will walk in the light of your face. 
\q1
\v 17 In your name will they rejoice all day long: 
\q2 and in your righteousness they will be exalted. 
\q1
\v 18 For you are the boast of their strength; 
\q2 and in your good pleasure our horn will be exalted, 
\q1
\v 19 for help is from the Lord, 
\q2 from the Holy One of Israel, our king. 
\q1
\v 20 Then you spoke in vision to your children, 
\q2 and said, I have laid help on a mighty one; 
\q2 I have exalted one chosen out of my people. 
\q1
\v 21 \x + \xo 88:21 \xt Acts 13. 22.\x*I have found David my servant; 
\q2 with my holy oil I have anointed him.
\q1
\v 22 For my hand will support him; 
\q2 and my arm will strengthen him. 
\q1
\v 23 The enemy will have no advantage against him; 
\q2 and the son of lawlessness will not hurt him again. 
\q1
\v 24 I will hew down his foes before him, 
\q2 and put to flight those that hate him. 
\q1
\v 25 But my truth and my mercy will be with him; 
\q2 and in my name his horn will be exalted. 
\q1
\v 26 I will set his hand in the sea, 
\q2 and his right hand in the rivers. 
\q1
\v 27 He will call upon me: You are my Father, 
\q2 my God, and the helper of my salvation. 
\q1
\v 28 I will make him firstborn, 
\q2 higher than the kings of the earth. 
\q1
\v 29 I will keep my mercy for him forever, 
\q2 and my covenant \add will be\add* faithful to him. 
\q1
\v 30 I will establish his seed forever and ever, 
\q2 and his throne as the days of heaven. 
\q1
\v 31 If his children should forsake my law, 
\q2 and not walk in my judgments; 
\q1
\v 32 if they should profane my ordinances, 
\q2 and not keep my commandments; 
\q1
\v 33 I will visit their transgressions with a rod, 
\q2 and their sins with scourges. 
\q1
\v 34 But I will not completely remove my mercy from him, 
\q2 nor will I do violence to my truth. 
\q1
\v 35 I will surely not profane my covenant; 
\q2 and I will surely not not reject what has gone out from my lips. 
\q1
\v 36 Once have I sworn by my holiness, 
\q2 that I will not lie to David. 
\q1
\v 37 His seed will endure forever, 
\q2 and his throne as the sun before me; 
\q1
\v 38 and as the moon, established forever, 
\q2 and as the faithful witness in heaven. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 39 But you have cast off and despised; 
\q2 you have rejected your anointed. 
\q1
\v 40 You have overthrown the covenant of your servant; 
\q2 you have profaned his sanctuary to the ground. 
\q1
\v 41 You have broken down all his defences; 
\q2 you have made his strongholds a terror. 
\q1
\v 42 All who go by the way have plundered him: 
\q2 he has become a reproach to his neighbours. 
\q1
\v 43 You have exalted the right hand of his enemies; 
\q2 you have made all his enemies rejoice. 
\q1
\v 44 You have turned back the help of his sword, 
\q2 and have not helped him in the battle. 
\q1
\v 45 You have brought him down from his purity;
\q2 you have cast his throne to the ground. 
\q1
\v 46 You have shortened the days of his time;
\q2 you have poured shame upon him. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 47 How long, O Lord, will you turn away, forever? 
\q2 Will your anger flame out as fire? 
\q1
\v 48 Remember what my being is: 
\q2 for have you created all the sons of men in vain? 
\q1
\v 49 What man is there who will live, and not see death, 
\q2 or deliver his soul from the hand of Hades? \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 50 Where are your ancient mercies, O Lord, 
\q2 which you swore to David in your truth? 
\q1
\v 51 Remember, O Lord, the reproach of your servants, 
\q2 which I have borne in my bosom from many nations; 
\q1
\v 52 with which your enemies have reviled, O Lord: 
\q2 with which they have reviled the recompence of your anointed.
\b
\q1
\v 53 Blessed be the Lord forever. 
\q0 So be it, so be it. 
\c 89 
\d
\v 1 A Prayer of Moses the man of God. 
\q1
\v 1b Lord, you have been our refuge in all generations. 
\q1
\v 2 Before the mountains existed, and the earth and the world were formed, 
\q2 even from age to age, You are. 
\q1
\v 3 Don't turn man back to humiliation. 
\q2 You said, Return, sons of men. 
\q1
\v 4 For a thousand years in your sight are as the yesterday which is past, 
\q2 and as a watch in the night. 
\q1
\v 5 Their nothingnesses will be years;
\q2 in the morning let them pass away as grass. 
\q1
\v 6 In the morning let it flower, and pass away: 
\q2 in the evening let it droop, let it be withered and dried up. 
\q1
\v 7 For we have perished in your anger, 
\q2 and in your wrath we have been troubled. 
\q1
\v 8 You have set our transgressions before you: 
\q2 our age is in the light of your face. 
\q1
\v 9 For all our days have gone, and we have passed away in your wrath: 
\q2 our years busied themselves like a spider. 
\q1
\v 10 The days of our years are seventy years; 
\q2 and if in strength, eighty years; 
\q2 and the greater part of them is labour and pain.
\q2 For gentleness has come upon us, and we will be chastened. 
\q1
\v 11 Who knows the power of your wrath,
\q2 and your anger from fear of you?
\q1
\v 12 Make your right hand known thus, 
\q2 and those who are bound in heart in wisdom.
\q1
\v 13 Return, O Lord! How long? 
\q2 And be entreated concerning your servants. 
\q1
\v 14 We have been satisfied in the morning with your mercy; 
\q2 and we exulted and rejoiced all our days. 
\q1
\v 15 We rejoiced in return for the days when you humbled us, 
\q2 the years in which we saw evils. 
\q1
\v 16 Look upon your servants, and upon your works; 
\q2 and guide their children. 
\q1
\v 17 Let the brightness of the Lord our God be upon us: 
\q2 direct the works of our hands for us.
\c 90 
\d
\v 1 Praise of a Song. Of David. 
\q1
\v 1b He who dwells in the help of the Highest, 
\q2 will sojourn under the shelter of the God of heaven. 
\q1
\v 2 He will say to the Lord, You are my helper and my refuge: 
\q2 my God; I will hope in him. 
\q1
\v 3 For he will deliver you from the snare of the hunters, 
\q2 from \add every\add* troublesome matter. 
\q1
\v 4 He will overshadow you with his shoulders, 
\q2 and you will trust under his wings: 
\q2 his truth will cover you with a shield. 
\q1
\v 5 You will not be afraid of terror by night; 
\q2 nor of the arrow flying by day; 
\q1
\v 6 of the thing that walks in darkness; 
\q2 of calamity, and the evil spirit at noon-day. 
\q1
\v 7 A thousand will fall at your side, 
\q2 and ten thousand at your right hand; 
\q2 but it will not come near you. 
\q1
\v 8 Only with your eyes will you observe 
\q2 and see the reward of sinners. 
\q1
\v 9 For you, O Lord, are my hope: 
\q2 you, my soul, have made the Most High your refuge. 
\q1
\v 10 No evils will come upon you, 
\q2 and no scourge will draw near your dwelling. 
\q1
\v 11 For he will give his angels charge concerning you, 
\q2 to keep you in all your ways. 
\q1
\v 12 \x + \xo 90:12 \xt Mat. 4. 6.\x*They will bear you up on their hands, 
\q2 lest at any time you dash your foot against a stone. 
\q1
\v 13 You will tread on the asp and basilisk: 
\q2 and you will trample on the lion and dragon. 
\q1
\v 14 For he has hoped in me, and I will deliver him: 
\q2 I will protect him, because he has known my name. 
\q1
\v 15 He will call upon me, and I will listen to him: 
\q2 I am with him in affliction; 
\q2 and I will deliver him, and glorify him. 
\q1
\v 16 I will satisfy him with length of days, 
\q2 and show him my salvation. 
\c 91 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of a Song for the Sabbath-day. 
\q1
\v 2 It is a good thing to give thanks to the Lord, 
\q2 and to sing praises to your name, O you Most High; 
\q1
\v 3 to proclaim your mercy in the morning, 
\q2 and your truth by night, 
\q1
\v 4 on a psaltery of ten strings, 
\q2 with a song on the harp. 
\q1
\v 5 For you, O Lord, have made me glad with your creation: 
\q2 and in the works of your hands I will exult. 
\q1
\v 6 How your works have been magnified, O Lord! 
\q2 Your thoughts are very deep. 
\q1
\v 7 A foolish man will not know, 
\q2 and a senseless man will not understand this. 
\q1
\v 8 When the sinners spring up as the grass, 
\q2 all the workers of lawlessness appear 
\q2 so they may be completely destroyed forever. 
\q1
\v 9 But you, O Lord, are most high forever. 
\q1
\v 10 For behold, your enemies will perish; 
\q2 and all the workers of lawlessness will be scattered. 
\q1
\v 11 But my horn will be exalted as that of a \f + \fr 91:11 \fqa Gr. \ft “one-horned” (μονοκέρωτος), traditionally “unicorn”; probably referring to powerful wild oxen or similar animals.\f*wild ox; 
\q2 and my old age with rich oil.
\q1
\v 12 My eye has seen my enemies, 
\q2 and my ear will hear the wicked that rise up against me. 
\q1
\v 13 The righteous will flourish as a palm-tree: 
\q2 he will be increased as the cedar in Lebanon. 
\q1
\v 14 Those who are planted in the house of the Lord 
\q2 will flourish in the courts of our God. 
\q1
\v 15 Then they will still increase in a \f + \fr 91:15 \fqa Gr. \ft πίονι, literally “fat”; a positive image suggesting richness, abundance, vigour, fertility, prosperity, and similar ideas.\f*fine old age; 
\q2 and they will be prosperous; 
\q2 so they may declare 
\q1
\v 16 that the Lord my God is upright, 
\q2 and there is no unrighteousness in him. 
\c 92 
\d
\v 1 For the day before the Sabbath. When the land had been settled. A praise song of David.
\q1
\v 1b The Lord reigns! 
\q2 He has clothed himself with honour: 
\q2 the Lord has clothed and girded himself with strength; 
\q2 for he has established the world, which will not be moved. 
\q1
\v 2 Your throne is prepared of old: 
\q2 you are from everlasting. 
\q1
\v 3 The rivers have lifted up, O Lord, 
\q2 the rivers have lifted up their voices, 
\q1
\v 4 at the voices of many waters: 
\q2 wonderful are the billows of the sea;
\q2 wonderful is the Lord in high places. 
\q1
\v 5 Your testimonies are made very sure: 
\q2 holiness becomes your house, O Lord, for length of days. 
\c 93 
\d
\v 1 A Psalm of David for the fourth day of the week.
\q1
\v 1b The Lord is a God of vengeances; 
\q2 the God of vengeances has declared himself. 
\q1
\v 2 Be exalted, you who judge the earth: 
\q2 give a reward to the proud. 
\q1
\v 3 How long will sinners, O Lord, 
\q2 how long will sinners boast? 
\q1
\v 4 They will utter and speak unrighteousness; 
\q2 all the workers of wickedness will speak.
\q1
\v 5 They have afflicted your people, O Lord, 
\q2 and hurt your heritage. 
\q1
\v 6 They have killed the widow and fatherless, 
\q2 and murdered the stranger. 
\q1
\v 7 They said, The Lord will not see, 
\q2 neither will the God of Jacob understand. 
\q1
\v 8 Understand now, you simple among the people; 
\q2 and you fools, at length be wise. 
\q1
\v 9 He who planted the ear, does he not hear? 
\q2 Or he who formed the eye, does not he perceive? 
\q1
\v 10 He who chastises the nations, will not he punish, 
\q2 he who teaches man knowledge? 
\q1
\v 11 \x + \xo 93:11 \xt 1 Cor. 3. 20.\x*The Lord knows the thoughts of men, 
\q2 that they are vain. 
\q1
\v 12 Blessed is the man whom you discipline, O Lord, 
\q2 and teach from your law; 
\q1
\v 13 to give him rest from evil days, 
\q2 until a pit is dug for the sinful one. 
\q1
\v 14 For the Lord will not cast off his people, 
\q2 neither will he forsake his inheritance; 
\q1
\v 15 until righteousness returns to judgment, 
\q2 and all the upright in heart cling to it. \qs Pause.\qs* 
\q1
\v 16 Who will rise up for me against the transgressors? 
\q2 Or who will stand up with me against the workers of wickedness? 
\q1
\v 17 If the Lord had not helped me, 
\q2 my soul would have almost sojourned in Hades. 
\q1
\v 18 If I said, My foot has been moved;
\q2 your mercy, O Lord, helped me.
\q1
\v 19 O Lord, according to the multitude of my griefs within my heart, 
\q2 your consolations loved my soul. 
\q1
\v 20 Will the throne of lawlessness have fellowship with you, 
\q2 which fashions misery by decree? 
\q1
\v 21 They will hunt for the soul of the righteous, 
\q2 and condemn innocent blood. 
\q1
\v 22 But the Lord was my refuge; 
\q2 and my God the helper of my hope. 
\q1
\v 23 He will recompense to them their lawlessness, 
\q2 and according to their wickedness
\q2 the Lord our God will completely destroy them. 
\c 94 
\d
\v 1 The praise of a Song. Of David.
\q1
\v 1b Come, let us exult in the Lord; 
\q2 let us raise a shout to God our Saviour. 
\q1
\v 2 Let us come before his presence with thanksgiving, 
\q2 and raise a shout to him with psalms. 
\q1
